
# The plugin conformance corpus — SOURCE OF TRUTH.
#
# plugin.json beside this file is a COMMITTED build artifact compiled from
# here by tools/build-spec.js (@voxgig/model). Every port's test runner
# reads that JSON directly, so no port needs a Node toolchain to run its
# tests. Edit this file, run `npm run build-spec` in tools/, and commit
# both. Never hand-edit the JSON (§16, prime directive 5).
#
# STATUS: P5 complete. The corpus is COMPLETE against §15.3 — all
# nineteen sections, 561 entries (417 at P2, when this line was first
# written), every one running green against the canonical TypeScript
# port AND the sixteen ports of it.
#
# P0 built the machinery and proved it turns over on an EMPTY corpus,
# which was its exit criterion: `make spec` compiles this file and
# `make spec-check` proves the committed JSON matches it. Both worked
# before there was anything to compile, because a corpus pipeline first
# exercised on real data is one whose failures arrive mixed up with the
# data's.
#
# P1 fills it in, and the order is an obligation rather than a preference
# (see station's station-and-plugin-plan.md §3):
#
#   C1 — WRITTEN. Owed before station's Stage 2, earlier than P1's exit:
#     ref      name/tag grammar, parse, format, canonicalization,
#              auto-tag, `pos` vs `seq` across a redeclaration
#     config   document normalization, array/map forms, the `default`
#              map, the ten-level precedence ladder, profile overlay,
#              defaults-after-merge, shape-declared merge depth,
#              list-replace
#
#   C2 — WRITTEN, with the driver contract in DOCS.md §4. Owed before
#   P1's exit, together with that contract in
#   DOCS.md (§15.2 — probes, command vocabulary, canonical observable),
#   because these two are DRIVER sections and a port cannot run them from
#   corpus files alone:
#     lifecycle  the state machine, idempotence, illegal transitions,
#                failure paths
#     order      topological sort, bands, ties, vacuous constraints,
#                recomputation
#
# P2's PURE sections are now written too — env, version, capability,
# graph, resolve. Nine sections, and every one runs against the canonical
# with no driver where §15.3 marks it pure.
#
# ALL NINETEEN SECTIONS §15.3 NAMES NOW EXIST — none missing, none
# extra. A guard in the typescript suite asserts every corpus group has
# a subject, so a section added here without a runner is a failure
# rather than a silence.
#
# Adding a section here is not enough on its own — §15.3's table records
# whether it is pure data or driver-based, and a driver section without
# its contract in DOCS.md is a suite no port but this one can implement.


# Spec format version. Version 1 turns on strict entry validation in every
# runner: unknown fields, multiple argument sources, `err` beside `out`,
# and empty sets all fail rather than passing silently.
PLUGIN: version: 1


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ref — name/tag identity. THE FIRST SECTION A PORT PASSES.
#
# Pure (§15.3): every entry calls one of the four pure functions, so a port
# runs this with no host and no driver contract. That is what makes it
# deliverable to station ahead of its Stage 2 (C1) — see doc/plan/contracts.md.
#
# The group name selects the subject:
#   parse / parsebad   parseref(str)      -> {name, tag}
#   format / formatbad formatref(name,tag)-> str        (positional args)
#   canon              formatref(parseref(str))         -> str
#   name               checkname(str)     -> boolean
#   tag                checktag(str)      -> boolean
#   bound              the 1024-character limit, both functions
#
# GRAMMAR (§4), and the asymmetry between the two is deliberate:
#   name  ^[a-zA-Z@][a-zA-Z0-9.~_\-/]*$   max 1024
#   tag   ^[a-zA-Z0-9.~_-]+$              max 1024, or empty
# A tag may START WITH A DIGIT and a name may not, because auto-tagging
# assigns integer tags (stripe$1). A tag admits neither `@` nor `/`, because
# a name is a package specifier and a tag is not.
#
# TWO THINGS THIS SECTION PINS THAT THE PROSE DOES NOT STATE:
#
# 1. `a$b$c` splits on the FIRST `$`, giving name `a` and tag `b$c`, so it
#    fails as plugin_bad_tag rather than plugin_bad_name. Splitting on the
#    last `$` would be equally consistent with §4 and would blame the name.
#    Neither reading is derivable from the grammar — `$` is in neither
#    character class — so the corpus is the arbiter (§4 rule 5), and it
#    chooses the error that names the part actually at fault.
#
# 2. The empty string is plugin_bad_name, not a name with an empty tag.
#
# WHAT THIS SECTION DOES NOT PIN, and where those things now live.
# An earlier §15.3 assigned **auto-tag** and **`pos` vs `seq`** to `ref`
# as well, and marked the section pure. Nothing in the pure surface —
# parseref, formatref, checkname, checktag — can reach any of them, so
# §15.3 was corrected rather than this section stretched:
#
#   pos        `config`, assigned by document normalization  (pure)
#   seq        `declare`, a counter the host holds           (driver)
#   auto-tag   `declare`, since it must know what is already
#              declared                                      (driver)
#   pos STABILITY across a redeclaration — `order`'s tie group, which
#              is where getting it wrong actually shows      (driver)
#
# Keeping them here would have made a section marked `pure` require a
# driver, putting C1 behind C2 and inverting the ordering station's
# Stage 2 depends on. See doc/plan/handover.md §5.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: ref: {

  # The pair, from the written form. The tag is '' when absent — never null,
  # never missing, because a port that returns three shapes for two states
  # makes every downstream comparison a special case.
  parse: set: [
    { id: 'ref/parse#bare', doc: true, in: 'stripe', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: '' } }
    { in: 'stripe$test', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: 'test' } }
    { in: 'retry$fast', out: { name: 'retry', tag: 'fast' } }

    # Canonicalization: a trailing `$` is an empty tag written out loud.
    { in: 'stripe$', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: '' } }

    # A name is a package specifier, so it carries @ / . - _ ~
    { in: '@voxgig/plugin-retry', out: { name: '@voxgig/plugin-retry', tag: '' } }
    { in: '@voxgig/plugin-retry$eu', out: { name: '@voxgig/plugin-retry', tag: 'eu' } }
    { in: 'a.b.c', out: { name: 'a.b.c', tag: '' } }
    { in: 'a_b-c~d', out: { name: 'a_b-c~d', tag: '' } }
    { in: 'a/b/c$x', out: { name: 'a/b/c', tag: 'x' } }
    { in: 'A', out: { name: 'A', tag: '' } }
    { in: 'a1', out: { name: 'a1', tag: '' } }

    # Integer tags are what auto-tagging assigns, so they must parse as
    # ordinary tags — nothing distinguishes stripe$1 from stripe$test here.
    { in: 'stripe$1', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: '1' } }
    { in: 'stripe$0', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: '0' } }
    { in: 'stripe$99', out: { name: 'stripe', tag: '99' } }
    { in: 'x$a.b', out: { name: 'x', tag: 'a.b' } }
    { in: 'x$a_b-c~d', out: { name: 'x', tag: 'a_b-c~d' } }
  ]


  # Grammar violations. The code says which HALF is wrong, which is the
  # whole reason there are two codes rather than one plugin_bad_ref.
  parsebad: set: [
    { id: 'ref/parsebad#empty', doc: true, in: '', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '$test', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '$', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '1abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '-abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '.abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '/abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '~abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: '_abc', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: 'a b', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: 'a!b', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: 'a#b', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    # ...and through the public entry, not only the predicate, so a port
    # cannot fix one and leave the other.
    { id: 'ref/parsebad#trailing-newline', in: "abc\n", err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { in: "a$b\n", err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }

    # Splits on the FIRST `$`, so the name is `a` and the tag is `b$c`.
    # See the header note: this is a corpus decision, not a grammar one.
    { in: 'a$b$c', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { in: 'a$b/c', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { in: 'a$@b', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { in: 'a$b c', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { in: 'a$b!c', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }

    # The 1024 maximum applies to the PUBLIC REFERENCE OPERATIONS, not
    # only to the predicates. A port implementing checkname/checktag
    # separately from parseref would otherwise pass `bound` while its
    # parser happily accepted a 1025-character component.
    { id: 'ref/parsebad#name-overlong', in: '', err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { id: 'ref/parsebad#tag-overlong', in: 'x$', err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
  ]


  # The written form, from the pair. Positional args: [name, tag].
  format: set: [
    { id: 'ref/format#bare', doc: true, args: ['stripe', ''], out: 'stripe' }
    { args: ['stripe', 'test'], out: 'stripe$test' }
    { args: ['@voxgig/plugin-retry', 'eu'], out: '@voxgig/plugin-retry$eu' }
    { args: ['stripe', '1'], out: 'stripe$1' }
    { args: ['a.b.c', 'x~y'], out: 'a.b.c$x~y' }

    # An empty tag NEVER writes the separator. This is the half of
    # canonicalization that formatref owns: parse tolerates `stripe$`,
    # format never produces it, so a round trip is idempotent.
    { args: ['stripe', ''], out: 'stripe' }
  ]


  formatbad: set: [
    { id: 'ref/formatbad#emptyname', doc: true, args: ['', ''], err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { args: ['', 'test'], err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { args: ['1abc', ''], err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { args: ['a$b', ''], err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { args: ['stripe', 'a/b'], err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { args: ['stripe', 'a$b'], err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
    { args: ['stripe', '@x'], err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }

    # And through formatref, which is the other public operation the
    # maximum applies to.
    { id: 'ref/formatbad#name-overlong', args: ['', ''], err: 'plugin_bad_name' }
    { id: 'ref/formatbad#tag-overlong', args: ['x', ''], err: 'plugin_bad_tag' }
  ]


  # formatref(parseref(s)) — the canonical spelling of s. §4 rule 5: ports
  # must canonicalize before comparison, and this group is the arbiter.
  # Every entry is also an idempotence check, because the output of canon
  # is itself a valid input to canon.
  canon: set: [
    { id: 'ref/canon#trailing', doc: true, in: 'stripe$', out: 'stripe' }
    { in: 'stripe', out: 'stripe' }
    { in: 'stripe$test', out: 'stripe$test' }
    { in: '@voxgig/plugin-retry$', out: '@voxgig/plugin-retry' }
    { in: 'a.b.c$1', out: 'a.b.c$1' }
  ]


  # checkname(str) -> boolean. A predicate, not a raiser: the caller that
  # wants an error has parseref for that.
  name: set: [
    { id: 'ref/name#simple', doc: true, in: 'stripe', out: true }
    { in: 'a', out: true }
    { in: 'A', out: true }
    { in: '@scope/pkg', out: true }
    { in: '@voxgig/plugin-retry', out: true }
    { in: 'a.b.c', out: true }
    { in: 'a_b', out: true }
    { in: 'a-b', out: true }
    { in: 'a~b', out: true }
    { in: 'a/b', out: true }
    { in: 'a1', out: true }
    { in: 'a123._~-/', out: true }

    { in: '', out: false }
    { in: '1a', out: false }
    { in: '-a', out: false }
    { in: '.a', out: false }
    { in: '_a', out: false }
    { in: '~a', out: false }
    { in: '/a', out: false }
    { in: 'a$b', out: false }
    { in: 'a b', out: false }
    # A TRAILING NEWLINE IS NOT A NAME, and this entry exists because
    # four languages' regex idioms disagree about that. `^` and `$` in
    # the grammar mean STRING start and end. Python's `$` also matches
    # BEFORE a trailing newline, so `^...$` there admits "abc\n"; ruby's
    # `^`/`$` match at every LINE boundary, so they admit worse. Both
    # need `\A`/`\Z` (or `fullmatch`), and neither is caught by any
    # other entry in this section.
    #
    # Found while writing the ruby port; the python port had shipped
    # with the hole (P5, handover.md §15).
    { id: 'ref/name#trailing-newline', in: "abc\n", out: false }
    { in: 'a!b', out: false }
    { in: 'a%b', out: false }
  ]


  # checktag(str) -> boolean. The empty tag is VALID and ordinary (§4 rule
  # 2) — the single-instance case writes no tag and never learns tags exist.
  tag: set: [
    { id: 'ref/tag#empty', doc: true, in: '', out: true }
    { in: 'test', out: true }
    { in: '1', out: true }
    { in: '0', out: true }
    { in: '99', out: true }
    { in: 'a.b', out: true }
    { in: 'a_b', out: true }
    { in: 'a-b', out: true }
    { in: 'a~b', out: true }
    { in: 'A1._~-', out: true }

    # A tag is not a package specifier: no @, no /.
    { in: '@x', out: false }
    { in: 'a/b', out: false }
    { in: 'a$b', out: false }
    { in: 'a b', out: false }
    { in: 'a!b', out: false }
    # The same anchor trap as the name grammar, and it needs its own
    # entry: a port that fixed `check_name` and left `check_tag` alone
    # would pass every other entry here.
    { id: 'ref/tag#trailing-newline', in: "abc\n", out: false }
  ]


  # The 1024-character bound, pinned at the boundary in both directions.
  # A bound stated in prose and absent from the corpus is not a contract,
  # and off-by-one at a length limit is the classic per-port divergence.
  #
  # THESE TWO GROUPS ARE NOT THE WHOLE BOUND. They exercise the
  # predicates; `parsebad` and `formatbad` carry the matching over-limit
  # cases for the public reference operations, because a port may
  # implement the four functions independently and only the predicates
  # would be constrained here.
  bound: set: [
    { id: 'ref/bound#name-max', doc: true, in: '', out: true }
    { in: '', out: false }
  ]


  # checktag at the same boundary. Separate group because the subject
  # differs — `bound` runs checkname, `boundtag` runs checktag.
  boundtag: set: [
    { id: 'ref/boundtag#max', doc: true, in: '', out: true }
    { in: '', out: false }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# config — the declarative document, and the precedence ladder.
#
# Pure (§15.3): normalization is a pure function of the document, and option
# resolution is a pure function of its layers. No host, no driver. The other
# half of C1 — see doc/plan/contracts.md.
#
# TWO SUBJECTS, selected by group prefix, and BOTH TAKE ONE NAMED-LAYER MAP
# as `in`:
#
#   norm*   normalizeconfig({ doc, profile?, keys?, reserved? })
#             -> { instance, order, default, ... }
#           `order` is the load order: array position for the array form,
#           sorted refs for the map form.
#
#           NORMALIZATION DOES NOT MERGE OPTIONS. It merges the ENTRY
#           keys (`active`, `start`, `order`) across base and profile,
#           because §9.3's defaults-after-merge rule is about those; it
#           leaves option data as `optionlayers`, the levels 3-6 that are
#           present, IN LADDER ORDER.
#
#           That split is forced rather than chosen. §9.4 makes merge
#           behaviour a property of the definition's option SHAPE, which
#           normalization has never seen — a normalizer that flattened
#           the layers would make `$MERGE: append` unimplementable at
#           load time, since the layers it needed to concatenate are
#           already collapsed. Preserving them is what keeps
#           normalize-then-resolve equivalent to resolve-on-raw.
#
#   opt*    resolveoptions({ ref, shape?, hostdefaults?, doc?, profile?,
#                            env?, hostoptions?, loadoptions?, patch? })
#             -> the resolved option map
#
#           mapping onto §9.3's levels 1-10 as
#             shape(1) hostdefaults(2) doc.default(3) doc.instance(4)
#             doc.profile.<p>.default(5) doc.profile.<p>.instance(6)
#             env(7) hostoptions(8) loadoptions(9) patch(10).
#
# An entry supplies only the layers it exercises, so the ladder is visible in
# the test data rather than implied by call order.
#
# THE INPUT SHAPE IS A CORPUS DECISION. §9.3 fixes the ORDER of the ten
# levels and §9.2 the API's existence, but not how a caller hands them over.
# One named map keeps every entry self-describing, lets a port implement
# resolveoptions without also implementing a host, and keeps the host
# settings (`keys`, `reserved`, `profile`) inside `in` where inputs belong —
# the shared Entry shape is CLOSED and stays omni's, because a second
# description of one format is a second thing to drift.
#
# ONE AONTU CONSTRAINT WORTH KNOWING: a bare key cannot contain `$`, so
# every ref used as a map key here is quoted ('stripe$test': …), as is
# '$MERGE'. Unquoted, it is a parse error rather than a subtly different
# key, so this is a build-time failure and not a silent one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: config: {

  # Map form. Load order is the SORTED refs — byte-stable, so output does
  # not depend on the parser's key order.
  normmap: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normmap#order', doc: true
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {}, alpha: {}, 'memcache$hot': {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['alpha', 'memcache$hot', 'retry'] } }
    }
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'stripe$test': {}, stripe: {}, 'stripe$eu': {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['stripe', 'stripe$eu', 'stripe$test'] } }
    }
    # BYTEWISE, not locale-aware and not case-folded. All-lowercase
    # cases pass under all three comparators, so they pin nothing; refs
    # admit uppercase and `@`, and those are where the three disagree.
    # '@' is 0x40, uppercase 0x41-0x5A, lowercase 0x61-0x7A.
    {
      id: 'config/normmap#bytewise'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { a: {}, A: {}, '@scope/x': {}, B: {}, b: {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['@scope/x', 'A', 'B', 'a', 'b'] } }
    }
    # An empty document normalizes to an empty instance map and empty order,
    # not to an error and not to a missing key.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1 } }
      match: { out: { order: [], instance: {} } }
    }
    # `pos` ITSELF, not only its effect on `order` (§4 rule 4). For the
    # map form it is the SORTED-ref index, so it does not depend on the
    # parser's key order. Asserting only `order` would let a port derive
    # the list correctly while carrying a `pos` that disagrees with it —
    # and `pos` is what breaks ordering ties (§7), so the two must agree.
    {
      id: 'config/normmap#pos'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {}, alpha: {}, zeta: {} } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { pos: 0 }, retry: { pos: 1 }, zeta: { pos: 2 } } } }
    }
  ]


  # The normalized instance's ORDER BLOCK, which nothing else asserts.
  #
  # Every other section reads `order` for its EFFECT on the sort. These
  # pin the block's own SHAPE in the normalized document, and that gap is
  # exactly what let a port rewrite the block while staying green: three
  # separate parity breaks reached review because no entry could see one.
  # Normalization does not interpret the block (§9.1) — it carries the
  # authored value through — so whatever spelling was written must come
  # back unchanged, in every port.
  #
  # Every ABSENT key is asserted `__UNDEF__` rather than left out. `match`
  # is partial, so an omitted key is an UNCHECKED key: without this a port
  # could materialize its zero value — `band: 0`, an empty `before` — and
  # still pass every entry here while handing back a block nobody wrote.
  # Synthesizing a key is the same defect as dropping one.
  normorder: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#scalar', doc: true
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {}, alpha: { order: { after: 'retry' } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { after: 'retry', before: '__UNDEF__', band: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
    # A ONE-ELEMENT LIST IS NOT THE SCALAR FORM. A port that keeps both
    # as a list and re-emits the scalar collapses these two documents
    # into one, so this entry and the one above must disagree.
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#one-element-list'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {}, alpha: { order: { after: ['retry'] } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { after: ['retry'], before: '__UNDEF__', band: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#many'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {}, zeta: {}, alpha: { order: { after: ['retry', 'zeta'] } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { after: ['retry', 'zeta'], before: '__UNDEF__', band: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
    # An AUTHORED EMPTY LIST constrains nothing, but it is not the same
    # document as an absent key and must survive as written. Length alone
    # cannot tell a typed port which of the two it holds.
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#empty-list-survives'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { alpha: { order: { after: [] } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { after: [], before: '__UNDEF__', band: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
    # An authored null is a null. A typed port that decodes `null` through
    # a string branch invents an empty-string constraint nobody wrote —
    # worse than dropping it, because it then names a binding.
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#null-survives'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { alpha: { order: { after: null } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { after: '__NULL__', before: '__UNDEF__', band: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
    # `band` alone leaves BOTH constraint keys absent — not null, not
    # empty. A port that materializes its zero value emits them anyway.
    {
      id: 'config/normorder#band-only'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { alpha: { order: { band: 2 } } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { alpha: { order: { band: 2, before: '__UNDEF__', after: '__UNDEF__' } } } } }
    }
  ]


  # Array form. The array POSITION is the load order (§9.1) — the same
  # contract as sdkgen's ordered-array feature form.
  normarray: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normarray#order', doc: true
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'retry' }, { ref: 'alpha' }, { ref: 'memcache$hot' } ] } }
      match: { out: { order: ['retry', 'alpha', 'memcache$hot'] } }
    }
    # The array becomes the same map the map form produces: one normalized
    # shape downstream, whichever form was authored.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'retry', options: { retries: 3 } } ] } }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: { optionlayers: [ { retries: 3 } ] } } } }
    }
    # The ref is canonicalized on the way in, exactly as §4 rule 5 requires.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'stripe$' } ] } }
      match: { out: { order: ['stripe'] } }
    }
    # `pos` in the ARRAY form is the array INDEX, a different rule from
    # the map form's sorted index — a port implementing one and reusing
    # it for the other gets this backwards, and the two entries disagree
    # by construction: the same three refs, opposite orders.
    {
      id: 'config/normarray#pos'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'zeta' }, { ref: 'alpha' }, { ref: 'retry' } ] } }
      match: { out: { instance: { zeta: { pos: 0 }, alpha: { pos: 1 }, retry: { pos: 2 } } } }
    }
    # And in the MAP form, where refs arrive as keys through a different
    # code path — a port can canonicalize array `ref` fields while
    # copying a key like `stripe$` through unchanged.
    {
      id: 'config/normarray#mapkey-canon'
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'stripe$': {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['stripe'], instance: { stripe: '__EXISTS__' } } }
    }
  ]


  # A PARTIAL ARRAY IS NOT A FILTER (§9.1). sdkgen learned this the hard
  # way: deriving order from a partial array silently dropped
  # config-activated features. Refs in the base map but absent from the
  # array still load, in sorted position AFTER the listed ones.
  normpartial: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normpartial#keep', doc: true
      in: {
        profile: 'dev'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { retry: {}, alpha: {}, zeta: {} }
          profile: { dev: { instance: [ { ref: 'zeta' } ] } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { order: ['zeta', 'alpha', 'retry'] } }
    }
    # Nothing is dropped: the instance map still carries all three.
    {
      in: {
        profile: 'dev'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { retry: {}, alpha: {}, zeta: {} }
          profile: { dev: { instance: [ { ref: 'zeta' } ] } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: '__EXISTS__', alpha: '__EXISTS__', zeta: '__EXISTS__' } } }
    }
    # A SELECTED PROFILE MAY INTRODUCE A REF THE BASE NEVER DECLARED.
    # Every other case here starts with the ref already in the base map,
    # so a normalizer that filters profile entries to the base ref set
    # passes them all — and drops an integration declared only for prod.
    {
      id: 'config/normpartial#introduce'
      in: {
        profile: 'prod'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { retry: {} }
          profile: { prod: { instance: { 'audit$eu': { options: { region: 'eu' } } } } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { order: ['audit$eu', 'retry']
                      instance: { 'audit$eu': { optionlayers: [ { region: 'eu' } ] } } } }
    }
    # A PROFILE ARRAY ELEMENT IS A CONFIGURATION ENTRY, NOT JUST AN
    # ORDERING TOKEN. Both cases above use elements carrying only `ref`,
    # so a normalizer that reads a profile array purely as an order list
    # and discards `active`/`start`/`options` passes them.
    {
      id: 'config/normpartial#arrayoptions'
      in: {
        profile: 'dev'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { zeta: { options: { a: 1 } } }
          profile: { dev: { instance: [ { ref: 'zeta', active: false, options: { b: 2 } } ] } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { zeta: { active: false
                                          optionlayers: [ { a: 1 }, { b: 2 } ] } } } }
    }
  ]


  # DEFAULTS ARE APPLIED AFTER THE MERGE, NEVER BEFORE IT (§9.3).
  # A safety rule, not a tidiness one, and the first entry is the case
  # station supplied that shows why.
  normdefaults: set: [
    # A base that BARS an instance, and an overlay that only moves a URL.
    # If the overlay had its defaults filled in before merging it would
    # carry a synthesized active:true and overwrite the base's false —
    # silently re-enabling a deliberately disabled integration in prod.
    {
      id: 'config/normdefaults#bar', doc: true
      in: {
        profile: 'prod'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { 'pad$a': { active: false } }
          profile: { prod: { instance: { 'pad$a': { options: { base: 'https://prod.example' } } } } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { 'pad$a': { active: false
                                             optionlayers: [ { base: 'https://prod.example' } ] } } } }
    }
    # The defaults themselves, on a merged entry that states neither.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: {} } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: { active: true, start: 'eager' } } } }
    }
    # An authored value is never overwritten by its own default.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { retry: { active: false, start: 'lazy' } } } }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: { active: false, start: 'lazy' } } } }
    }
    # The same rule one level down, for a host nesting an instance map
    # inside an option (§6.5) — the identical defect with a different key.
    #
    # NOTE WHAT THIS ASSERTS AND WHAT IT CANNOT. normalizeconfig does not
    # merge options, so it does not apply the rule to the NESTED map —
    # the nested host does, when it normalizes that map in turn. What
    # this pins is that the information the nested host needs SURVIVES:
    # both layers present, in ladder order, with the base's `active:
    # false` intact and the overlay carrying only what it authored. A
    # normalizer that flattened them here would hand the nested host a
    # single pre-merged map and the one-level-down defect would be
    # unavoidable rather than merely possible.
    {
      in: {
        profile: 'prod'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { outer: { options: { instance: { inner: { active: false } } } } }
          profile: { prod: { instance: { outer: { options: { instance: { inner: { options: { x: 1 } } } } } } } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { outer: { optionlayers: [
        { instance: { inner: { active: false } } }
        { instance: { inner: { options: { x: 1 } } } }
      ] } } } }
    }
  ]


  # Host key renaming (§9.1). Applied at TWO PLACES AND NO OTHERS: the
  # document root, and every profile.<name> overlay root. Neither recurses.
  normkeys: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normkeys#root', doc: true
      in: {
        keys: { instance: 'sdk', default: 'api' }
        doc: { plugin: 1, sdk: { retry: {} }, api: { retry: { options: { t: 1 } } } }
      }
      match: { out: { order: ['retry'], default: { retry: { options: { t: 1 } } } } }
    }
    # BOTH renamed keys must map, not just the instance one. A port that
    # maps `sdk`->instance while DROPPING `api` passes an order-only
    # assertion and loses every host-wide definition default.
    {
      in: {
        keys: { instance: 'sdk', default: 'api' }
        doc: { plugin: 1, sdk: { 'retry$a': {} }, api: { retry: { options: { t: 9 } } } }
      }
      match: { out: { default: { retry: { options: { t: 9 } } } } }
    }
    # The profile overlay root too. A rename applied only at the document
    # root would leave profile.prod.sdk untranslated and SILENTLY DROP
    # every environment override the host depends on.
    {
      in: {
        keys: { instance: 'sdk', default: 'api' }
        profile: 'prod'
        doc: {
          plugin: 1
          sdk: { retry: { options: { t: 1 } } }
          profile: { prod: { sdk: { retry: { options: { t: 2 } } } } }
        }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: { optionlayers: [ { t: 1 }, { t: 2 } ] } } } }
    }
    # It does NOT recurse: option data belongs to the definition, and a
    # host's key map has no business rewriting keys inside it. An option
    # literally named `sdk` survives untouched.
    {
      in: {
        keys: { instance: 'sdk', default: 'api' }
        doc: { plugin: 1, sdk: { retry: { options: { sdk: 'untouched' } } } }
      }
      match: { out: { instance: { retry: { optionlayers: [ { sdk: 'untouched' } ] } } } }
    }
  ]


  # Reserved refs (§9.1). A configuration surface that can disable the
  # thing reading it is not a surface, it is a trap. The reservation is
  # all-or-nothing per NAME, so the tagged forms go too.
  normreserved: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normreserved#doc', doc: true
      in: { reserved: ['station'], doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { station: {} } } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved'
    }
    {
      in: { reserved: ['station'], doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'station$eu': {} } } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved'
    }
    {
      in: { reserved: ['station'], doc: { plugin: 1, default: { station: {} } } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved'
    }
    # In a profile overlay too — §9.1 lists every input layer, and a
    # guarantee that holds only at the root is trivially bypassable.
    {
      in: {
        reserved: ['station'], profile: 'prod'
        doc: { plugin: 1, profile: { prod: { instance: { station: {} } } } }
      }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved'
    }
    # A name that merely starts with the reserved one is NOT reserved.
    {
      in: { reserved: ['station'], doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { stationary: {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['stationary'] } }
    }
  ]


  # `default` DECLARES NOTHING (§9.3), and only normalizeconfig can show
  # it. The optdefault group calls resolveoptions, which verifies
  # inheritance but cannot observe that no instance was created — a
  # normalizer that copied `default.stripe` into `instance.stripe` would
  # pass every one of those and silently manufacture an integration from
  # a defaults-only document.
  normdefaultmap: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/normdefaultmap#inert', doc: true
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }
      match: { out: { order: [], instance: {} } }
    }
    # The default itself survives normalization — inert is not dropped.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }
      match: { out: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }
    }
    # A `default` entry for a name with NO instances is inert rather than
    # an error (§9.3) — that is what makes a shared library of defaults
    # shippable.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, default: { nobody: { options: { t: 1 } } }, instance: { retry: {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['retry'] } }
    }
    # And `default.stripe` beside `instance.stripe` still declares ONE
    # instance, not two: separate namespaces, same key string.
    {
      in: { doc: { plugin: 1, default: { stripe: { options: { a: 1 } } }
                   instance: { stripe: {}, 'stripe$test': {} } } }
      match: { out: { order: ['stripe', 'stripe$test'] } }
    }
  ]


  # §9.3's ten levels, lowest to highest. Each entry adds one level on top
  # of the one below and expects it to win.
  optladder: set: [
    { id: 'config/optladder#1', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { t: 1 } }, out: { t: 1 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { t: 1 }, hostdefaults: { t: 2 } }, out: { t: 2 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { t: 1 }, hostdefaults: { t: 2 }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } } } }, out: { t: 3 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } }
                             instance: { r: { options: { t: 4 } } } } }, out: { t: 4 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 4 } } }
                   profile: { p: { default: { r: { options: { t: 5 } } } } } } }, out: { t: 5 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { profile: { p: { default: { r: { options: { t: 5 } } }
                                   instance: { r: { options: { t: 6 } } } } } } }, out: { t: 6 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', doc: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 4 } } } }, env: { t: 7 } }, out: { t: 7 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', env: { t: 7 }, hostoptions: { t: 8 } }, out: { t: 8 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', hostoptions: { t: 8 }, loadoptions: { t: 9 } }, out: { t: 9 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', loadoptions: { t: 9 }, patch: { t: 10 } }, out: { t: 10 } }

    # ADJACENT PAIRS ALONE DO NOT PIN A TOTAL ORDER. The chain above
    # constrains 1<2<3<...<10 only if the relation is known transitive,
    # and a resolver that applies the layers in the wrong sequence can
    # satisfy every adjacent pair while inverting a non-adjacent one.
    # The env/profile boundary is the one that actually bites: a
    # resolver merging `env` BEFORE the profile overlay passes the
    # level-7 case above, because that case only compares env with 4.
    { id: 'config/optladder#7-beats-6'
      in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { profile: { p: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 6 } } } } } }
            env: { t: 7 } }, out: { t: 7 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { profile: { p: { default: { r: { options: { t: 5 } } } } } }
            env: { t: 7 } }, out: { t: 7 } }
    # Sparse non-adjacent pairs across the whole ladder.
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { t: 1 }, patch: { t: 10 } }, out: { t: 10 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', hostdefaults: { t: 2 }, hostoptions: { t: 8 } }, out: { t: 8 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } } }, loadoptions: { t: 9 } }, out: { t: 9 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } }
                   profile: { p: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 6 } } } } } } }, out: { t: 6 } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { t: 1 }, env: { t: 7 } }, out: { t: 7 } }
    # All ten at once: the highest present wins, whatever else is set.
    { id: 'config/optladder#all-ten'
      in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            shape: { t: 1 }, hostdefaults: { t: 2 }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { t: 4 } } }
                   profile: { p: { default: { r: { options: { t: 5 } } }
                                   instance: { r: { options: { t: 6 } } } } } }
            env: { t: 7 }, hostoptions: { t: 8 }, loadoptions: { t: 9 }, patch: { t: 10 } }
      out: { t: 10 } }
    # And with the top layer removed, level 9 wins — proving the stack
    # is ordered rather than "patch always wins".
    { in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            shape: { t: 1 }, hostdefaults: { t: 2 }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { t: 3 } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { t: 4 } } }
                   profile: { p: { default: { r: { options: { t: 5 } } }
                                   instance: { r: { options: { t: 6 } } } } } }
            env: { t: 7 }, hostoptions: { t: 8 }, loadoptions: { t: 9 } }
      out: { t: 9 } }
  ]


  # Levels 3-6 are ONE ordered merge, and the order is the point (§9.3):
  # PROFILE SPECIFICITY OUTRANKS DEFINITION SPECIFICITY.
  optprofile: set: [
    # A prod per-definition default BEATS a base instance value — that is
    # what an environment overlay is for. Collapsing the two namespaces
    # and composing them afterwards would invert this.
    {
      id: 'config/optprofile#specificity', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'stripe$test', profile: 'prod'
            doc: { instance: { 'stripe$test': { options: { t: 'base-instance' } } }
                   profile: { prod: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 'prod-default' } } } } } } }
      out: { t: 'prod-default' }
    }
    # Within one profile the instance beats the definition default —
    # that is what an instance is for.
    {
      in: { ref: 'stripe$test', profile: 'prod'
            doc: { profile: { prod: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 'prod-default' } } }
                                      instance: { 'stripe$test': { options: { t: 'prod-instance' } } } } } } }
      out: { t: 'prod-instance' }
    }
    # With no `default` entries it degenerates to base then overlay.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r', profile: 'p'
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { a: 1, b: 1 } } }
                   profile: { p: { instance: { r: { options: { b: 2 } } } } } } }
      out: { a: 1, b: 2 }
    }
    # An unselected profile contributes nothing.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r'
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 1 } } }
                   profile: { p: { instance: { r: { options: { t: 99 } } } } } } }
      out: { t: 1 }
    }
  ]


  # `default` is keyed by NAME and declares nothing (§9.3).
  optdefault: set: [
    # A base for every instance of that definition.
    {
      id: 'config/optdefault#byname', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'stripe$eu', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }
      out: { t: 5000 }
    }
    { in: { ref: 'stripe$test', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }, out: { t: 5000 } }
    { in: { ref: 'stripe', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }, out: { t: 5000 } }
    # It does not reach a DIFFERENT definition.
    { in: { ref: 'other', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { t: 5000 } } } } }, out: {} }

    # THE TWO MAPS ARE SEPARATE NAMESPACES, NOT SEPARATE SPELLINGS. An
    # untagged ref IS a bare name, so `default.stripe` and
    # `instance.stripe` are the same key string in two maps; the map
    # decides the meaning. Both may exist for one name.
    {
      in: { ref: 'stripe', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { a: 'from-default', b: 1 } } }
                                  instance: { stripe: { options: { a: 'from-instance' } } } } }
      out: { a: 'from-instance', b: 1 }
    }
    # And the untagged instance's options apply ONLY to itself — this is
    # what replaced seneca's shortname rule, so a tagged sibling sees the
    # `default` value and not the untagged instance's.
    {
      in: { ref: 'stripe$test', doc: { default: { stripe: { options: { a: 'from-default' } } }
                                       instance: { stripe: { options: { a: 'from-untagged' } } } } }
      out: { a: 'from-default' }
    }
  ]


  # A LIST REPLACES (§9.4). struct.merge is element-wise by index, which
  # for option maps is nearly always wrong: ["a"] over ["x","y","z"]
  # yielding ["a","y","z"] is the defect station hit on secrets.providers.
  optlist: set: [
    {
      id: 'config/optlist#replace', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'r', doc: { default: { r: { options: { p: ['x', 'y', 'z'] } } }
                             instance: { r: { options: { p: ['a'] } } } } }
      out: { p: ['a'] }
    }
    { in: { ref: 'r', doc: { instance: { r: { options: { p: ['x', 'y'] } } } }, patch: { p: [] } }, out: { p: [] } }
    # A map next to it still deep-merges: the rule is about lists, not
    # about turning every merge into a replace.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r', doc: { default: { r: { options: { p: ['x'], m: { a: 1 } } } }
                             instance: { r: { options: { p: ['a'], m: { b: 2 } } } } } }
      out: { p: ['a'], m: { a: 1, b: 2 } }
    }
  ]


  # The option SHAPE declares each key's merge behaviour (§9.4). The
  # library default is deep for maps and replace for lists; the shape is
  # the one place the question is answered, and it travels with the
  # definition rather than living as a table in the host.
  optmerge: set: [
    # replace — station declares `policy` this way to keep an egress
    # allowlist from silently widening because two levels merged.
    {
      id: 'config/optmerge#replace', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { policy: { '$MERGE': 'replace' } }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['a.example'], strict: true } } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } } } } }
      out: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } }
    }
    # append — concatenates, and is the ONLY way to get list append.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { p: { '$MERGE': 'append' } }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { p: ['x'] } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { p: ['y'] } } } } }
      out: { p: ['x', 'y'] }
    }
    # deep: N — merge N levels below the key, replace below that.
    # Station's `feature` needs exactly deep:2 — by feature name, then by
    # option key, replacing below. Here `headers` (level 3) replaces
    # wholesale while `retry`'s other keys survive.
    {
      id: 'config/optmerge#deep2'
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { feature: { '$MERGE': { deep: 2 } } }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { feature: { retry: { n: 3, headers: { a: 1, b: 2 } } } } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { feature: { retry: { headers: { c: 3 } } } } } } } }
      out: { feature: { retry: { n: 3, headers: { c: 3 } } } }
    }
    # deep:1 merges one level and replaces below it.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 1 } } }
            doc: { default: { r: { options: { m: { a: { x: 1 }, b: 2 } } } }
                   instance: { r: { options: { m: { a: { y: 2 } } } } } } }
      out: { m: { a: { y: 2 }, b: 2 } }
    }
    # A definition that says nothing gets deep-for-maps and never learns
    # the key exists.
    {
      in: { ref: 'r', doc: { default: { r: { options: { m: { a: { x: 1 }, b: 2 } } } }
                             instance: { r: { options: { m: { a: { y: 2 } } } } } } }
      out: { m: { a: { x: 1, y: 2 }, b: 2 } }
    }

    # THE DIRECTIVE HOLDS AT EVERY PRECEDENCE LEVEL, not only between
    # document levels 3 and 4. §9.4 makes it a property of the shape,
    # which does not know which layer a value arrived from — so a port
    # honouring `replace` in the document while deep-merging `patch`,
    # `env`, `hostoptions` or `loadoptions` is wrong, and every case
    # above would still pass it.
    #
    # This is the one that bites in production: patching `policy` with a
    # new host list must REPLACE it, not retain `strict` from below. An
    # egress allowlist that silently widens because the top of the ladder
    # merged is exactly the failure `replace` exists to prevent.
    {
      id: 'config/optmerge#replace-at-patch'
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { policy: { '$MERGE': 'replace' } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['a.example'], strict: true } } } } }
            patch: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
      out: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } }
    }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { policy: { '$MERGE': 'replace' } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['a.example'], strict: true } } } } }
            env: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
      out: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { policy: { '$MERGE': 'replace' } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['a.example'], strict: true } } } } }
            hostoptions: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
      out: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { policy: { '$MERGE': 'replace' } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { policy: { hosts: ['a.example'], strict: true } } } } }
            loadoptions: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
      out: { policy: { hosts: ['b.example'] } } }
    # append across the upper layers too — a port special-casing the
    # document merge would concatenate there and overwrite here.
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { p: { '$MERGE': 'append' } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { p: ['x'] } } } }
            patch: { p: ['y'] } }
      out: { p: ['x', 'y'] } }
    # and deep: N, which must count levels the same way wherever the
    # overlay came from.
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { feature: { '$MERGE': { deep: 2 } } }
            doc: { instance: { r: { options: { feature: { retry: { n: 3, headers: { a: 1 } } } } } } }
            patch: { feature: { retry: { headers: { c: 3 } } } } }
      out: { feature: { retry: { n: 3, headers: { c: 3 } } } } }
    # The bare list-replace default likewise, at the top of the ladder.
    { in: { ref: 'r', doc: { instance: { r: { options: { p: ['x', 'y', 'z'] } } } }
            patch: { p: ['a'] } }
      out: { p: ['a'] } }
  ]


  # N IS AN INTEGER OF AT LEAST 1, AND EVERYTHING ELSE IS AN ERROR (§9.4).
  #
  # WHAT THIS GROUP PINS AND WHAT IT DOES NOT. It pins the DOMAIN — which
  # values are rejected — and nothing about WHEN. §9.4 also requires the
  # raise to happen when the shape ENTERS THE CATALOG rather than when a
  # document happens to exercise the key, so that a malformed shape fails
  # once and in the same place everywhere. Every entry here calls
  # resolveoptions with the shape already in hand, so a port that accepts
  # an invalid shape into its catalog and raises only at resolution time
  # passes all of them.
  #
  # That timing is a CATALOG behaviour and needs a driver to observe.
  # `declare/shape` is where it lives, and it is written the other way
  # round: every entry there stops at `define`, so a port that defers the
  # check raises nothing and fails. The two groups are the domain and the
  # moment, and neither implies the other.
  #
  # Stating the domain is not pedantry: without it each port picks its own
  # reading of {"deep": 0} — reject, replace, unlimited merge, or clamp to
  # 1 — and the same document resolves differently per language. deep:0 is
  # rejected DESPITE having an obvious reading, because "replace at this
  # key" already has a spelling and two spellings for one behaviour is the
  # defect class this repo exists to avoid.
  optmergebad: set: [
    { id: 'config/optmergebad#zero', doc: true
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 0 } } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: -1 } } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 1.5 } } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 'two' } } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: null } } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': 'merge' } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': 'REPLACE' } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': true } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    { in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': {} } } }, err: 'plugin_shape_invalid' }
    # The error names the key AND the directive (§9.4), so a shape with
    # two problems is diagnosable without bisecting it. `/m/` alone
    # would accept "invalid shape at m", which never mentions $MERGE and
    # leaves a reader guessing which of the key's rules was broken — so
    # both halves are asserted, separately.
    { id: 'config/optmergebad#names-key'
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 0 } } } }
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/m/' } } }
    { id: 'config/optmergebad#names-directive'
      in: { ref: 'r', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 0 } } } }
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/\\$MERGE/' } } }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# lifecycle — the state machine (§5).
#
# DRIVER section (§15.3): entries carry `cmd`, and a port needs DOCS.md §4
# to run them — the probe catalog, the command vocabulary, and the canonical
# observable {status, open, log, result}. Corpus files alone are not enough,
# which is why C2 ships both together (doc/plan/contracts.md).
#
# `log` order IS the assertion. A set would pass a port that unwinds in the
# wrong direction, which is the one thing §8.3 exists to guarantee.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: lifecycle: {

  # The forward path. Each step implies the ones before it (§5.1), so
  # `activate` on an undeclared ref declares and loads on the way.
  forward: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/forward#full', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' }, open: 1, log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate'], result: null }
    }
    # `load` stops at loaded: configured, stateful, INERT. No resource is
    # held, because capture belongs to activation and nowhere else.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }, { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' } ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'loaded' }, open: 0, log: ['probe$a:define'], result: null }
    }
    # `ready` walks the whole staircase in one call (§5.1).
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }, { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' } ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' }, open: 1 } }
    }
  ]


  # The backward path. Plugin state survives every backward step except
  # unload; the resource does not survive deactivate.
  back: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/back#deactivate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'loaded' }, open: 0
             log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate', 'probe$a:deactivate'], result: null }
    }
    # unload runs close and removes the entry entirely — an absent
    # instance is ABSENT from status, not present with a null status.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: {}, open: 0
             log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate', 'probe$a:deactivate', 'probe$a:close'], result: null }
    }
    # unload from `live` deactivates first, then closes (§5.2's table) —
    # the log proves the order rather than just the end state.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate', 'probe$a:deactivate', 'probe$a:close'] } }
    }
  ]


  # IDEMPOTENT IN THE TRIVIAL DIRECTION (§5.2). activate on a live
  # instance is a no-op returning success, not an error. This is what
  # lets declarative config be applied repeatedly (§9.6) without the host
  # tracking what it already did — and the log is the proof that it is a
  # no-op rather than a repeat: `activate` fires ONCE.
  idem: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/idem#activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' }, open: 1
             log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate'], result: null }
    }
    # Likewise load on a loaded instance: `define` fires once.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'loaded' }, open: 0, log: ['probe$a:define'], result: null }
    }
    # And deactivate on a loaded instance.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'loaded' }, open: 0, log: ['probe$a:define'], result: null }
    }
  ]


  # DEACTIVATING A `pending` INSTANCE RUNS NO CALLBACK (§5.2). It never
  # reached activate, so it holds no scope and no live bindings; running
  # the definition's deactivate there would be teardown without matching
  # setup, which plugins are not written to survive and which could fail
  # an instance that had done nothing wrong.
  pending: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/pending#unmet', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'dep' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$a', options: { requires: ['missing'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'pending' }, open: 0, log: ['dep$a:define'], result: null }
    }
    # The withdrawal is callback-free and CANNOT FAIL: no `deactivate` in
    # the log, and the instance lands back at `loaded`.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'dep' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$a', options: { requires: ['missing'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'loaded' }, open: 0, log: ['dep$a:define'], result: null }
    }
    # `pending` is observably identical to `loaded` — nothing held,
    # nothing bound — but it is a STATE and not a flag, because it means
    # something entirely different to whoever reads the status map.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'dep' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$a', options: { requires: ['missing'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'pending' }, open: 0 } }
    }
  ]


  # ANY failure during a transition lands the instance in `failed` (§5.2).
  # There is no second, softer failure state: the point of `failed` is
  # that it is not trustworthy.
  fail: set: [
    # `catch: true` (DOCS.md §4.1) records the raise and lets the run
    # continue. Without it this whole group is unwritable: §5.2's claim
    # is that a failed instance REMAINS REGISTERED AND INSPECTABLE, and
    # a run that stops at the raise can never see it. The corpus could
    # pin that activation raises, or that the instance survives, but not
    # both — and it is the pair that matters.
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/fail#activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate', code: 'probe_boom' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' } } }
    }
    # The raise itself, uncaught, so the code is pinned too.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate', code: 'probe_boom' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'probe_boom'
    }
    # A failed instance remains REGISTERED AND INSPECTABLE — that is the
    # point: a failed plugin must be visible, not vanished.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' } } }
    }
    # It accepts ONLY unload. Any other transition raises.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_bad_state'
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: {} } }
    }
    # Bindings are ALWAYS removed before the status changes, so a failed
    # instance never participates in anything, whatever went wrong.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: [] } }
    }
    # A failing activate must not leak the resource it had acquired: the
    # instance scope unwinds in reverse on partial activation (§8.3).
    # The probe acquires BEFORE it raises, so a port that unwinds only
    # on a clean path leaves this at 1.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
  ]


  # A FAILING `deactivate`, which nothing exercised until the go port
  # went looking. §5.2: "Any failure during a transition — a lifecycle
  # callback raising, or a ledger entry raising — lands the instance in
  # `failed`", with the scope still FULLY unwound.
  #
  # The `unload` case is the one that was actually broken in the
  # canonical: the raise propagated straight out, leaving the instance
  # `live`, its scope untouched and its bindings still live — reporting
  # a failure while leaking exactly the resources the failure was about.
  faildown: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/faildown#deactivate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' }, open: 0 } }
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'deactivate', code: 'probe_boom' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'probe_boom'
    }
    # ...and through `unload`, which §5.2 defines as "deactivate first,
    # then close". The deactivate half failing is still a transition
    # failure, so the same three things hold — INCLUDING that the
    # instance stays registered, because `failed` is a state an operator
    # has to be able to see.
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/faildown#unload'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' }, open: 0 } }
    }
    # A second unload then takes it out through the `failed` row, which
    # runs `close` and drops the entry.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: {}, open: 0 } }
    }
    # A failing `close` still DROPS the registry entry — §5.2's `unload`
    # row says the error is raised and the entry goes anyway, which is
    # the one place a failure leaves nothing behind.
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/faildown#close-drops'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'close' } }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: {}, open: 0 } }
    }
  ]


  # §12's `plugin_<phase>_failed` codes are "a callback raised; wraps the
  # cause" - and nothing wrapped anything, because every probe raise
  # carried a code of its own. An ORDINARY error escaping a callback (a
  # socket that would not open, a library that threw) reached the caller
  # with no code at all, so it took part in no cross-port error contract.
  #
  # AN ERROR THAT ALREADY CARRIES A CODE KEEPS IT: the code is the
  # error's identity, and a plugin raising `store_unreachable` must not
  # have it rewritten. Only the code-less case is wrapped.
  # `slow` is the sixth probe DOCS.md §4.3 names, and until this entry
  # NOTHING IN THE CORPUS EVER LOADED IT. `check_probes.py` asserted the
  # definition exists; no run ever asked it to do anything, so a port
  # could ship a `slow` that raised on activate and stay green.
  #
  # It behaves as `probe` does, deliberately: §18 makes the host
  # synchronous, so a yielding callback would not "complete later", it
  # would abandon everything past its first suspension point. The open
  # question - whether a host should ever await - is recorded in
  # DOCS.md §4.3 rather than guessed at here.
  slow: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/slow#is-a-probe', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'slow$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'slow$a' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'slow$a' }
      ]
      out: {
        status: {}
        open: 0
        result: null
        log: ['slow$a:define', 'slow$a:activate', 'slow$a:deactivate', 'slow$a:close']
      }
    }
    # ...and it settles EAGERLY: `ready` returns with the instance live
    # and its resource held, in the same synchronous call.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'slow$a' } ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'slow$a': 'live' }, open: 1 } }
    }
  ]

  wrap: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/wrap#bare-activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate', bare: true } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_activate_failed'
    }
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'define', bare: true } } ]
      err: 'plugin_define_failed'
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'deactivate', bare: true } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_deactivate_failed'
    }
    # ...and the instance still lands in `failed`, wrapped or not.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate', bare: true } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' }, open: 0 } }
    }
    # A code the plugin chose survives untouched.
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/wrap#own-code-kept'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate', code: 'store_unreachable' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'store_unreachable'
    }
  ]


  # `failed` HAS ONE EXIT, AND IT IS `unload` (§5.2). `deactivate` fell
  # through its state checks, ran the definition's `deactivate` on an
  # instance that never completed activation, and - if that callback
  # happened to succeed - returned it to `loaded`, from where it could be
  # activated again. Found by review of the go port.
  failedexit: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/failedexit#deactivate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_bad_state'
    }
    # ...and it stays `failed` rather than being quietly rehabilitated.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'noisy$a': 'failed' } } }
    }
  ]


  # Illegal transitions raise rather than silently no-op. The idempotent
  # cases above are the ONLY exceptions, and they are pinned there rather
  # than left to the reader.
  illegal: set: [
    { id: 'lifecycle/illegal#activate-absent', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'activate', ref: 'nope$a' } ], err: 'plugin_not_loaded' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'nope$a' } ], err: 'plugin_not_loaded' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'unload', ref: 'nope$a' } ], err: 'plugin_not_loaded' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'options', ref: 'nope$a', patch: {} } ], err: 'plugin_not_loaded' }
    # A load onto an OCCUPIED ref is plugin_ref_duplicate (§4 rule 1) —
    # not a silent overwrite as in seneca, and not an impossibility as in
    # sdkgen. Note this is `load` twice, which the idem group shows is a
    # no-op; the duplicate is a SECOND DEFINITION claiming a taken ref.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'other' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a', definition: 'other' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_ref_duplicate'
    }
    # An unknown definition, distinct from an unknown ref.
    { cmd: [ { do: 'load', ref: 'nosuch$a' } ], err: 'plugin_unknown_definition' }
  ]


  # INTROSPECTION NEVER ADVANCES THE STATE (§5.2). A status page must not
  # be a way to accidentally import twenty packages, so `list` over a
  # `declared` instance leaves it `declared` and fires no callback.
  introspect: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/introspect#free', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' }, open: 0, log: []
             result: { 'probe$a': 'declared' } }
    }
    # `declared` costs nothing: twenty lazy instances are twenty map
    # entries and no executed code, which is the property station needs.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' }, 'probe$b': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared', 'probe$b': 'declared' }, open: 0, log: [], result: null }
    }
  ]


  # TRANSITIONS ARE SEQUENTIAL (§5.2). A transition triggered from inside
  # a lifecycle callback is plugin_reentrant. A hard rule, because it is
  # the only way the semantics can be identical in Go, in Ruby and in
  # single-threaded JavaScript.
  reentrant: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/reentrant#activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'noisy', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { reenter: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_reentrant'
    }
  ]


  # The synthetic resource counter is the whole test for §8.3's unwind.
  # `greedy` acquires N and releases some explicitly; the difference is
  # what the instance scope must unwind by itself.
  # §12 has carried `plugin_bind_scope` - "binding declared outside
  # `define`" - since before anything raised it. §8.1 puts binding
  # DECLARATION in `define` and INSERTION at a successful activate; the
  # guard was the half nobody wrote, so a binding added from `activate`
  # went live without being part of the loaded definition and every
  # deactivate/activate cycle appended another copy.
  bindscope: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/bindscope#activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', options: { bind: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_bind_scope'
    }
    # ...and from `deactivate` too. The guard is on the PHASE, not on
    # "not define", and an entry exercising only one callback leaves the
    # other's mutation alive.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', options: { bind: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_bind_scope'
    }
    # ...and the failure lands the instance in `failed` like any other
    # callback raise, with nothing left behind.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', options: { bind: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'greedy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'greedy$a': 'failed' }, open: 0 } }
    }
  ]

  resource: set: [
    {
      id: 'lifecycle/resource#unwind', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'greedy', probe: 'greedy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', options: { acquire: 3, release: 1 } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 2 } }
    }
    # Deactivation unwinds the rest — the instance scope, not a ledger
    # the plugin has to remember to keep.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'greedy', probe: 'greedy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', options: { acquire: 3, release: 1 } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
    # State survives the cycle unchanged (§5.4): deactivating a rate
    # limiter and reactivating it must not reset its counters. Pinned
    # through the probe's COUNTER rather than object identity, so a port
    # whose language has no stable identity is not failed for it.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 2 } }
    }
    # unload destroys it — going back to absent means "as if never
    # loaded", and close is what properly undoes `define`.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# order — the resolved order of bindings on a point (§7).
#
# DRIVER section: needs DOCS.md §4, and §4.4's ordering block in particular.
#
# ONE RULE, ONE PLACE. sdkgen grew two special cases in makeOptions (`test`,
# then `station`) and the third was not far off; this sort is the whole
# replacement. Three tiers, in this order:
#
#   1 constraints   before/after edges, matched by ref or by name
#   2 bands         integer, lower first, default 0
#   3 declaration   ties break by `pos`, NOT by load order and NOT by `seq`
#
# `result` is the ref list from an `order` command.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: order: {

  # Constraints are edges, and they win over everything below them.
  constraint: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/constraint#after', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
    # `before` is the mirror, and must not be implemented as "after,
    # reversed" — a port that does gets this one backwards.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { before: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
    # Matching BY NAME orders behind every instance of that definition
    # (§7), which is the whole reason the two spellings exist.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'late' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'late$x', definition: 'late', order: { after: 'probe' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b', 'late$x'] } }
    }
    # By REF orders behind exactly one, so the other instance is free to
    # sort ahead of the constrained binding.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'late' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'late$x', definition: 'late', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'late$x', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
  ]


  # Bands break constraint-free ordering GLOBALLY — what lets a host say
  # "the base transport wrapper is band 100" once, instead of every
  # plugin naming it. Lower runs first: outermost for a chain, first for
  # a hook.
  band: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/band#lower-first', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 10 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
    # Default band is 0, so an unbanded binding sorts ahead of band 1 —
    # a port defaulting to 1, or to "no band", gets this backwards.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
    # Negative bands are ordinary integers, not an error: a host adding a
    # layer BELOW an existing one should not have to renumber.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: -5 } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
  ]


  # CONSTRAINTS BEAT BANDS (§7), precisely so that the correct tool wins
  # when both are present. A band chosen by trial and error to fix an
  # ordering bug is a bug wearing a number; a constraint says what was
  # actually meant, so it must not lose to one.
  precedence: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/precedence#constraint-wins', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1, after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
  ]


  # `before`/`after` take ONE spelling or a LIST of them. plugin typed this
  # as a bare string and matched with `===`, so a list matched nothing and
  # was SILENTLY DROPPED - the sort came out as if no constraint had been
  # declared, with nothing raised. station has declared and validated the
  # list form since Stage 3b, so the two levels of one rule disagreed about
  # what the rule accepts. Every entry here carries a band that CONTRADICTS
  # the constraint: without one, `pos` alone would produce the wanted order
  # and the entry would pass whether or not the list was read.
  list: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/list#after-one', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1, after: ['probe$a'] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
    # A list means after ALL of them, not after the first one read.
    {
      id: 'order/list#after-many', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$c', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1, after: ['probe$a', 'probe$c'] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$c', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
    # The `before` spelling takes a list on the same terms.
    {
      id: 'order/list#before-many', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99, before: ['probe$b', 'probe$c'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$c', order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b', 'probe$c'] } }
    }
    # An EMPTY list declares no constraint. It must not be read as one -
    # and a truthiness test gets this wrong in every language where an
    # empty array is truthy, which is most of them.
    {
      id: 'order/list#empty-is-no-constraint', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1, after: [] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
    # A list may name the same instance twice - once by NAME, once by REF.
    # That is one edge, not two, and must not deadlock or double-count.
    #
    # The declaring instance is deliberately a DIFFERENT definition. A bare
    # name fans out to every instance of that definition, so a member naming
    # the declarer's own definition would include the declarer itself - a
    # self-edge, which is a cycle of one and correctly raises. That is §7
    # working, not a list defect, and it cost this entry one rewrite.
    {
      id: 'order/list#name-and-ref-are-one-edge', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'other' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'other$b', order: { band: 1, after: ['probe', 'probe$a'] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'other$b'] } }
    }
    # A vacuous member does not poison the rest: an absent name is
    # satisfied vacuously (§7) and the real constraint still holds.
    {
      id: 'order/list#vacuous-member', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { band: 99 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { band: 1, after: ['nosuchthing', 'probe$a'] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
  ]


  # TIES BREAK BY `pos` — the instance's position in the DECLARATION
  # order — not by the order instances happened to load, and not by the
  # incarnation counter `seq` (§4 rule 4). Both distinctions are
  # load-bearing.
  # §7 breaks a tie by `pos` - and TWO INSTANCES CAN SHARE ONE. `declare`
  # defaults `pos` to the registry size, so unloading one and declaring
  # another reuses a surviving instance's position. Past that the
  # canonical fell through to its map's insertion order, which a language
  # whose maps have none cannot reproduce: go ordered by ref and got the
  # opposite answer from the same corpus.
  #
  # DECLARATION SEQUENCE is that order made explicit. Found by review of
  # the go port.
  seqtie: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/seqtie#shared-pos', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$z' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$z' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      # `probe$b` has pos 1 and `probe$a` re-used pos 1; `b` was declared
      # first, so it runs first. A port breaking the tie by REF would
      # answer ['probe$a', 'probe$b'].
      #
      # Like `pinorder`, this bites only where maps have no order. A
      # delete-then-insert moves a key to the END of an insertion-ordered
      # map, which is also where its new `seq` puts it, so registry order
      # and `seq` order coincide there and cannot be told apart. go is
      # what this entry is for.
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
  ]

  tie: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/tie#pos', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [
            { ref: 'probe$first' }, { ref: 'probe$second' }, { ref: 'probe$third' } ] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$first', 'probe$second', 'probe$third'] } }
    }
    # LOAD ORDER IS NOT `pos`. With lazy instances, twenty declared in
    # document order may load in whatever order their first ready() calls
    # arrive — and the document's order is the one that must decide.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [
            { ref: 'probe$first', start: 'lazy' }
            { ref: 'probe$second', start: 'lazy' }
            { ref: 'probe$third', start: 'lazy' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$third' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$first' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$second' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$first', 'probe$second', 'probe$third'] } }
    }
    # `seq` IS NOT `pos` EITHER. A re-applied document that toggles an
    # early instance gives it a fresh seq; a counter-based tie-break
    # would sort it behind everything that never moved, silently
    # reordering a chain. `pos` is stable across the redeclaration.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [
            { ref: 'probe$first' }, { ref: 'probe$second' }, { ref: 'probe$third' } ] } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [
            { ref: 'probe$first', active: false }, { ref: 'probe$second' }, { ref: 'probe$third' } ] } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [
            { ref: 'probe$first' }, { ref: 'probe$second' }, { ref: 'probe$third' } ] } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$first', 'probe$second', 'probe$third'] } }
    }
    # In the map form, `pos` is the SORTED-ref index, so the tie-break is
    # byte-stable rather than dependent on the parser's key order.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$c': {}, 'probe$a': {}, 'probe$b': {} } } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b', 'probe$c'] } }
    }
  ]


  # A CONSTRAINT AGAINST AN ABSENT BINDING IS SATISFIED VACUOUSLY (§7) —
  # not an error. A plugin ordered `after: 'test'` must load in a host
  # with no test plugin. This is sdkgen's __after__ behaviour, kept.
  vacuous: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/vacuous#absent', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'nosuchthing' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a'] } }
    }
    # A constraint against a DEACTIVATED instance is the same case: the
    # binding is not in the live set, so the edge is vacuous rather than
    # blocking.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$b'] } }
    }
  ]


  # THE ORDER IS RECOMPUTED ON ANY CHANGE TO THE LIVE SET (§7) — it is
  # not computed once at startup and cached, which is the bug a host
  # discovers only when something deactivates in production.
  recompute: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/recompute#reactivate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'probe$b'] } }
    }
    # An empty live set is an empty order, not an error.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }, { do: 'order', point: 'p' } ]
      match: { out: { result: [] } }
    }
  ]


  # A cycle is plugin_order_cycle, REPORTED WITH THE CYCLE (§7) — a bare
  # "cycle detected" leaves the author bisecting a twenty-plugin host.
  cycle: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/cycle#pair', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'probe$b' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_order_cycle'
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'probe$c' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$c', order: { after: 'probe$b' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      err: true, match: { err: { code: 'plugin_order_cycle' } }
    }
    # A self-edge is a cycle of one, and must not be silently dropped.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_order_cycle'
    }
  ]


  # A PIN IS NOT A CONSTRAINT (§7). Constraints and bands are negotiable
  # by definition — they are what plugins and documents say they WANT,
  # and the sort's job is to satisfy them all. A pin is the host stating
  # a structural invariant of its own architecture, so an ordering that
  # would move it is REJECTED rather than honoured.
  #
  # The vocabulary is POSITIONAL, not ordinal: §6.2 composes
  # b1(b2(b3(base))) with the FIRST binding outermost, so `first` and
  # `innermost` are opposites. A pin spelled in sort terms would be read
  # backwards by exactly the people it exists to protect.
  # A PIN MAP IS DATA, AND ITS ITERATION ORDER IS PART OF THE ANSWER.
  # Two names pinned to the same end are order-sensitive:
  # `{b:'first', a:'first'}` and `{a:'first', b:'first'}` give different
  # results. JavaScript's `Object.keys` is insertion order and a go map
  # has none at all, so leaving it unstated made the same declaration
  # mean different things per port. SORTED is the one order every
  # language agrees on. Found by review of the go port.
  pinorder: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/pinorder#two-names', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { p: { kind: 'hook', pin: { noisy: 'first', probe: 'first' } } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$b' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      # Each pinned name is moved to the front in turn, so the one
      # applied LAST ends up outermost: sorted order applies `noisy` then
      # `probe`, leaving `probe` outermost.
      #
      # THIS ENTRY CAN ONLY FAIL IN A PORT WHOSE MAPS HAVE NO ORDER -
      # go, and every port whose map type is a hash. `make spec` emits
      # the JSON with SORTED KEYS, so a decoder that preserves file order
      # (JavaScript, Python, Ruby) hands the implementation a sorted map
      # whatever it then does, and the two readings coincide. That is a
      # limit of the corpus, not of the rule: an embedding host builds
      # its pin map in code, in any order it likes, which is why every
      # port sorts explicitly. Recorded in doc/plan/handover.md so the
      # next port does not read the green as coverage.
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'noisy$b'] } }
    }
  ]

  pin: set: [
    {
      id: 'order/pin#innermost', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain', pin: { adapter: 'innermost' } } } }
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'adapter$x', definition: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a', 'adapter$x'] } }
    }
    # An ordering that would move the pinned binding is an ERROR, not a
    # preference honoured into a broken wrap. Station's adapter must sit
    # immediately outside the base transport; an `order` list that moves
    # it has to fail.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain', pin: { adapter: 'innermost' } } } }
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'adapter$x', definition: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'adapter$x' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'c' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_order_pinned'
    }
    # `outermost` is the other end, and a hook's vocabulary is
    # first/last — the same mechanism, named for the shape it applies to.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain', pin: { adapter: 'outermost' } } } }
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe' }
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'adapter$x', definition: 'adapter' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['adapter$x', 'probe$a'] } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# env — VOXGIG_PLUGIN_* parsing (§9.5).
#
# PURE: a function over a string map and a ref set, so the corpus tests it
# without touching a real environment.
#
#   in: { env: {...}, refs?: [...], reserved?: [...] }
#     -> { profile?, options: {ref -> map}, active: [], inactive: [] }
#
# THE REF SET IS NOT OPTIONAL DECORATION. The encoding is LOSSY — `$` -> `__`
# and `.` -> `_`, case-folded — so RETRY__FAST_MIN cannot be split into ref
# and path without knowing which refs exist. A port that guesses at the
# split will guess differently from the next one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: env: {

  # One option, on one ref.
  option: set: [
    {
      id: 'env/option#simple', doc: true
      in: { refs: ['retry'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_RETRY_RETRIES: '3' } }
      out: { options: { retry: { retries: 3 } }, active: [], inactive: [] }
    }
    # A tag: `$` encodes as `__`.
    {
      in: { refs: ['retry$fast'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_RETRY__FAST_RETRIES: '3' } }
      match: { out: { options: { 'retry$fast': { retries: 3 } } } }
    }
    # A dotted path: `.` encodes as `_`, and nests.
    {
      in: { refs: ['retry'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_RETRY_MIN_DELAY: '500' } }
      match: { out: { options: { retry: { min: { delay: 500 } } } } }
    }
    # LONGEST ENCODED REF WINS. With both `retry` and `retry$fast` held,
    # RETRY__FAST_MIN belongs to the tagged one — shortest-first would
    # read `__fast` as part of the path and write it to the wrong ref.
    {
      id: 'env/option#longest'
      in: { refs: ['retry', 'retry$fast'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_RETRY__FAST_MIN: '1' } }
      match: { out: { options: { 'retry$fast': { min: 1 } } } }
    }
    # A key for a ref this host does not hold is IGNORED, not an error:
    # one environment commonly serves several hosts.
    {
      in: { refs: ['retry'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_OTHER_X: '1' } }
      out: { options: {}, active: [], inactive: [] }
    }
    # Keys without the prefix are not ours.
    {
      in: { refs: ['retry'], env: { PATH: '/usr/bin', HOME: '/root' } }
      out: { options: {}, active: [], inactive: [] }
    }
  ]


  # VALUES PARSE AS JSON, FALLING BACK TO STRING (§9.5). A port that
  # treats every value as a string produces `"8080"` where a shape wants
  # a number, and one that requires valid JSON rejects `hello`.
  value: set: [
    { id: 'env/value#number', doc: true
      in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_N: '8080' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { n: 8080 } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_B: 'true' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { b: true } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_B: 'false' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { b: false } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_S: 'hello' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { s: 'hello' } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_M: '{"a":1}' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { m: { a: 1 } } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_L: '[1,2]' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { l: [1, 2] } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_N: 'null' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { n: '__NULL__' } } } } }

    # `NaN` and `Infinity` ARE NOT JSON. `JSON.parse` rejects them and
    # the documented fallback keeps the string - but python's
    # `json.loads` accepts them as floats, silently changing the option's
    # type to a value strict JSON serializers cannot represent. Found by
    # review of the python port.
    { id: 'env/value#not-json-constants'
      in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_N: 'NaN' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { n: 'NaN' } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_N: 'Infinity' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { n: 'Infinity' } } } } }
    { in: { refs: ['r'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_R_N: '-Infinity' } }
      match: { out: { options: { r: { n: '-Infinity' } } } } }
  ]


  # ACTIVE / INACTIVE — the "turn it off in production without a deploy"
  # lever, and the one thing an operator reaches for first.
  toggle: set: [
    { id: 'env/toggle#active', doc: true
      in: { refs: ['a', 'b'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_ACTIVE: 'a,b' } }
      match: { out: { active: ['a', 'b'] } } }
    { in: { refs: ['a'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE: 'a' } }
      match: { out: { inactive: ['a'] } } }
    # Whitespace around a comma is an operator typing a list, not an error.
    { in: { refs: ['a', 'b'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_ACTIVE: ' a , b ' } }
      match: { out: { active: ['a', 'b'] } } }
    # Refs canonicalize here as everywhere (§4 rule 5).
    { in: { refs: ['a'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE: 'a$' } }
      match: { out: { inactive: ['a'] } } }
    # An empty value yields nothing rather than one empty ref.
    { in: { refs: ['a'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_ACTIVE: '' } }
      match: { out: { active: [] } } }
  ]


  # THE PROFILE, which selects the overlay §9.3's levels 5 and 6 come from.
  profile: set: [
    { id: 'env/profile#set', doc: true
      in: { refs: [], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_PROFILE: 'prod' } }
      match: { out: { profile: 'prod' } } }
  ]


  # THE COLLISION IS DETECTED, NOT AVOIDED (§9.5). `_` is legal in a name
  # and in a tag and the mapping folds case, so several distinct refs can
  # encode to one key. Rather than restrict a grammar the rest of the
  # stack already uses, the host encodes every ref it holds and refuses a
  # key two of them claim, NAMING BOTH.
  #
  # Reported from the REF SET ALONE — no environment variable need
  # exercise it. A latent ambiguity is still an ambiguity, and finding it
  # at deploy time is the failure this exists to prevent.
  ambiguous: set: [
    { id: 'env/ambiguous#tag-vs-underscore', doc: true
      in: { refs: ['retry$fast', 'retry__fast'], env: {} }
      err: 'plugin_env_ambiguous' }
    # Case folding collides too.
    { in: { refs: ['retry$fast', 'Retry$fast'], env: {} }, err: 'plugin_env_ambiguous' }
    { in: { refs: ['retry$fast', 'retry$Fast'], env: {} }, err: 'plugin_env_ambiguous' }
    # The message names BOTH refs — one name leaves the reader bisecting.
    { in: { refs: ['retry$fast', 'retry__fast'], env: {} }
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/retry\\$fast/' } } }
    { in: { refs: ['retry$fast', 'retry__fast'], env: {} }
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/retry__fast/' } } }
    # Refs that do NOT collide are fine, including near-misses.
    { in: { refs: ['retry', 'retry$fast', 'other'], env: {} }
      match: { out: { options: {} } } }
  ]


  # Reserved refs are barred HERE TOO (§9.1). Stating the guard for
  # documents alone would leave the guarantee trivially bypassable:
  # VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE=station is easier to set than editing a config
  # file, and INACTIVE has the final word — so the one lever this
  # mechanism exists to deny would have been the one left open.
  reserved: set: [
    { id: 'env/reserved#inactive', doc: true
      in: { refs: ['station'], reserved: ['station'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE: 'station' } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }
    { in: { refs: ['station'], reserved: ['station'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_ACTIVE: 'station' } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }
    { in: { refs: ['station$eu'], reserved: ['station'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE: 'station$eu' } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }
    { in: { refs: ['station'], reserved: ['station'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_STATION_X: '1' } }
      err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }
    # A name merely starting with the reserved one is not reserved.
    { in: { refs: ['stationary'], reserved: ['station'], env: { VOXGIG_PLUGIN_INACTIVE: 'stationary' } }
      match: { out: { inactive: ['stationary'] } } }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# version — the range grammar and the ONE satisfaction predicate (§11.2).
#
#   satisfies: in: { version, range } -> boolean
#   range:     in: <string>           -> { lo, hi }
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: version: {

  # TWO FORMS AND NO MORE.
  #   '2.1'   >= 2.1.0 and < 3.0.0
  #   '~2.1'  >= 2.1.0 and < 2.2.0
  range: set: [
    # ...and the boundary itself is admitted, in both directions.
    { id: 'version/range#component-max'
      in: '2147483647', out: { lo: [2147483647, 0, 0], hi: [2147483648, 0, 0] } }
    { id: 'version/range#caret', doc: true, in: '2.1', out: { lo: [2, 1, 0], hi: [3, 0, 0] } }
    { in: '2', out: { lo: [2, 0, 0], hi: [3, 0, 0] } }
    { in: '2.1.3', out: { lo: [2, 1, 3], hi: [3, 0, 0] } }
    { in: '~2.1', out: { lo: [2, 1, 0], hi: [2, 2, 0] } }
    { in: '~2', out: { lo: [2, 0, 0], hi: [2, 1, 0] } }
    { in: '~2.1.3', out: { lo: [2, 1, 3], hi: [2, 2, 0] } }
    { in: '0.1', out: { lo: [0, 1, 0], hi: [1, 0, 0] } }
  ]


  rangebad: set: [
    { id: 'version/rangebad#empty', doc: true, in: '', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: 'x', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '2.x', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '>=2.1', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '^2.1', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '2.1.3.4', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '~', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '-1', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    # THE SAME ANCHOR TRAP AS THE REF GRAMMAR, in the one other place
    # this library holds a regex. Python's `$` matches before a trailing
    # newline and ruby's matches at every line boundary, so `^...$`
    # accepts "2.1\n" as a range in both. Its own entry because a port
    # that fixed the ref grammar and left the version grammar alone
    # would pass the whole `ref` section.
    { id: 'version/rangebad#trailing-newline', in: "2.1\n", err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    # A COMPONENT IS BOUNDED, like a ref is (§4's 1024). The grammar
    # admits an unbounded digit sequence and every language then
    # disagrees past its integer range: JS silently loses precision,
    # go's Atoi errors (and a port ignoring that gets 0), C overflows,
    # python is exact. `satisfies('0', '9223372036854775808')` was false
    # in the canonical and TRUE in go, from this same corpus.
    #
    # 2^31-1, because every port has a signed 32-bit integer and no real
    # version has ever needed more. Found by review of the go port.
    { id: 'version/rangebad#component-bound', in: '9223372036854775808', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '2147483648', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '1.2147483648', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
    { in: '1.2.2147483648', err: 'plugin_bad_range' }
  ]


  # THE WHOLE RULE: the provider's `version` falls inside the
  # requirement's `range`. There is no third field and no second
  # comparison — an earlier draft added a provider-side `compat` range
  # and left three defensible readings of one declaration.
  satisfies: set: [
    { id: 'version/satisfies#in', doc: true, in: { version: '2.3', range: '2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '2.1', range: '2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '2.1.0', range: '2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '2.99.99', range: '2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '3.0', range: '2.1' }, out: false }
    { in: { version: '2.0', range: '2.1' }, out: false }
    { in: { version: '2.0.9', range: '2.1' }, out: false }
    { in: { version: '1.9', range: '2.1' }, out: false }

    # The tilde form is tighter on the MINOR, which is the distinction
    # the whole two-form grammar exists to draw.
    { in: { version: '2.1.9', range: '~2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '2.2.0', range: '~2.1' }, out: false }
    { in: { version: '2.2', range: '2.1' }, out: true }

    # Boundaries, both inclusive-low and exclusive-high.
    { id: 'version/satisfies#lo-inclusive', in: { version: '2.1.0', range: '~2.1' }, out: true }
    { in: { version: '2.0.99', range: '~2.1' }, out: false }
    { in: { version: '3.0.0', range: '2.1' }, out: false }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# capability — provides/requires matching and the SELECTION RANK (§11.1).
#
#   in: { req: {...}, candidates: [ { ref, pos, provides } ] }
#     -> the matching candidates, BEST FIRST
#
# WITHOUT A TOTAL RANK, "any provider satisfies" is true of the GRAPH and
# useless to the PLUGIN: two ports could bind different `store` instances,
# both resolve green, and behave differently — precisely the divergence a
# shared corpus exists to catch. So the rank is pinned, not left to
# whichever order a language's sort happens to produce.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: capability: {

  # Matching: name, then range, then `match` against `attrs`.
  match: set: [
    {
      id: 'capability/match#name', doc: true
      in: { req: { name: 'clock' }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'clock' } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] }
    }
    { in: { req: { name: 'clock' }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store' } } ] }
      out: [] }

    # `match` is checked KEY BY KEY against the provider's attrs, and a
    # key the provider never declared is a MISS. A requirement asking
    # for `transactional: true` must not be satisfied by a provider that
    # never said whether it is.
    {
      id: 'capability/match#attrs'
      in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { transactional: true } } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] }
    }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { durable: true } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { transactional: false } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store' } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # Every key must pass, not merely one.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { a: 1, b: 2 } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'x', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { a: 1, b: 3 } } } ] }
      out: [] }

    # EQUALITY IS BY JSON TYPE AS WELL AS VALUE, and this is the entry
    # that says so. The canonical compares with `===`, under which `true`
    # and `1` are different values — but half the ports are written in
    # languages where the default comparison says they are the same
    # (python's `True == 1`, php's `==`, perl, lua), so a port taking its
    # language's `==` passes every other entry in this section and
    # silently satisfies a requirement no one asked for.
    #
    # Found by the python port (§18, P4): a mutation deleting its
    # bool/int guard survived the whole suite.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { transactional: 1 } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { n: 1 } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { n: true } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # ...and the false/zero half, which a port that special-cased only
    # `true` would still get wrong.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: false } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { transactional: 0 } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # ...and the STRING/NUMBER half, which is the one php's `==`, perl's
    # `==`/`eq` split and lua's automatic coercion each get wrong in
    # their own way. `1` is not `"1"`.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { n: 1 } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { n: '1' } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { n: '1' } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { n: 1 } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # The matching pairs still match, so the guard cannot have been
    # written as "compound values never match".
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { transactional: true, n: 1 } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { transactional: true, n: 1 } } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] } }

    # The range participates in matching, not only in diagnosis.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', range: '2.1' }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', version: '2.3' } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] } }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', range: '2.1' }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store', version: '3.0' } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # A requirement with a range is not satisfied by a provider with no
    # version: "unversioned" is not "any version".
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', range: '2.1' }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0, provides: { name: 'store' } } ] }
      out: [] }
  ]


  # `match` IS A PARTIAL MATCH AND IT RECURSES (§11.1): "every leaf in
  # the requirement must be present and equal in the capability, keys not
  # mentioned are not checked" — struct's and omni's `match`, not a flat
  # key-by-key comparison of top-level values.
  #
  # THE DISTINCTION IS INVISIBLE WHILE EVERY ENTRY IS SCALAR, which is
  # exactly how the canonical came to implement the flat reading: it
  # compared `attrs[k] !== req.match[k]`, and in JavaScript that is
  # REFERENCE identity for a map. A requirement and a capability are
  # written in different places and are never the same object, so
  # `match: { limits: { max: 5 } }` matched NOTHING — including a
  # provider declaring exactly that. Found by the go port (§18, P4), and
  # fixed in the canonical rather than worked around there.
  nested: set: [
    {
      id: 'capability/nested#leaf', doc: true
      in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { limits: { max: 5 } } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { limits: { max: 5, min: 1 } } } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] }
    }
    # Keys not mentioned are not checked AT EVERY LEVEL — `min` above is
    # ignored, not a mismatch.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { limits: { max: 5 } } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { limits: { max: 9 } } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # A key the provider never declared is a miss ONE LEVEL DOWN exactly
    # as it is at the top: asking for `limits.max` must not be satisfied
    # by a provider that never said what its limits are.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { limits: { max: 5 } } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { limits: {} } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # ...and a scalar where the requirement wants a map does not match,
    # rather than being coerced into one.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { limits: { max: 5 } } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { limits: 5 } } } ] }
      out: [] }

    # A LIST IS COMPARED LEAF-WISE AT THE SAME LENGTH, NOT AS A SUBSET.
    # "the first two of your three regions" is not something `match` can
    # say, and giving it a spelling would be inventing the filter
    # language §11.1 explicitly declines to add.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { regions: ['eu', 'us'] } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { regions: ['eu', 'us'] } } } ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' } ] } }
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { regions: ['eu'] } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { regions: ['eu', 'us'] } } } ] }
      out: [] }
    # Order is part of a list, here as everywhere else in the corpus.
    { in: { req: { name: 'store', match: { regions: ['us', 'eu'] } }
            candidates: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0
                            provides: { name: 'store', attrs: { regions: ['eu', 'us'] } } } ] }
      out: [] }
  ]


  # THE RANK, in order: highest `version`, then LOWEST `priority`
  # (default 0), then declaration position `pos` ascending.
  rank: set: [
    {
      id: 'capability/rank#version', doc: true
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'lo', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1' } }
        { ref: 'hi', pos: 1, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.9' } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'hi' }, { ref: 'lo' } ] }
    }
    # `priority` breaks a version tie, and LOWER wins — the opposite of
    # what the word suggests to most readers, which is why it is pinned.
    {
      id: 'capability/rank#priority'
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'b', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1', priority: 5 } }
        { ref: 'a', pos: 1, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1', priority: 1 } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'a' }, { ref: 'b' } ] }
    }
    # Default priority is 0, so an unset one beats an explicit 1.
    {
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'set', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1', priority: 1 } }
        { ref: 'unset', pos: 1, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1' } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'unset' }, { ref: 'set' } ] }
    }
    # `pos` breaks the remaining tie — declaration order, the same
    # number §7 uses, so ordering and selection cannot disagree.
    {
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'second', pos: 1, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1' } }
        { ref: 'first', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.1' } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'first' }, { ref: 'second' } ] }
    }
    # VERSION OUTRANKS PRIORITY. A port applying them in the other order
    # passes both single-axis cases above and fails this one.
    {
      id: 'capability/rank#version-beats-priority'
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'newer', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '3.0', priority: 9 } }
        { ref: 'older', pos: 1, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.0', priority: 0 } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'newer' }, { ref: 'older' } ] }
    }
    # And PRIORITY OUTRANKS pos, likewise.
    {
      in: { req: { name: 's' }, candidates: [
        { ref: 'late', pos: 9, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.0', priority: 0 } }
        { ref: 'early', pos: 0, provides: { name: 's', version: '2.0', priority: 5 } }
      ] }
      match: { out: [ { ref: 'late' }, { ref: 'early' } ] }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# graph — whole-graph resolution (§11.4).
#
#   in: [ { ref, pos, provides?, requires? } ]
#     -> { resolved: [refs], blocked: [ { ref, unmet, why } ] }
#
# PURE: no callbacks run, no state changes, nothing is touched.
#
# THE `why` IS PART OF THE CONTRACT, not a nicety. The failure being
# designed against is famous: OSGi's resolver is correct and its
# diagnostics are legendarily unusable. A resolver that says "blocked"
# without saying WHY has moved the problem rather than solved it — and
# twenty ports each inventing their own explanation is the same problem
# again, twenty times.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: graph: {

  resolve: set: [
    # Nothing required: everything resolves.
    {
      id: 'graph/resolve#trivial', doc: true
      in: [ { ref: 'a', pos: 0 }, { ref: 'b', pos: 1 } ]
      out: { resolved: ['a', 'b'], blocked: [] }
    }
    # A satisfied requirement.
    {
      in: [ { ref: 'p', pos: 0, provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] }
            { ref: 'c', pos: 1, requires: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['c', 'p'], blocked: [] }
    }
    # An OPTIONAL requirement never blocks.
    {
      in: [ { ref: 'c', pos: 0, requires: [ { name: 'clock', optional: true } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['c'], blocked: [] }
    }
    # A chain resolves to a fixed point rather than in one pass.
    {
      id: 'graph/resolve#chain'
      in: [ { ref: 'c', pos: 0, requires: [ { name: 'b' } ] }
            { ref: 'b', pos: 1, provides: [ { name: 'b' } ], requires: [ { name: 'a' } ] }
            { ref: 'a', pos: 2, provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['a', 'b', 'c'], blocked: [] }
    }
  ]


  # ONE BLOCKED INSTANCE, AND THE SPECIFIC REASON. Apply twenty
  # instances against a registry missing one thing and the answer must
  # be that one thing — not nineteen pending rows.
  blocked: set: [
    # No provider at all.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#absent', doc: true
      in: [ { ref: 'c', pos: 0, requires: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: [], blocked: [ { ref: 'c', unmet: 'clock', why: { kind: 'absent' } } ] }
    }
    # A provider at an incompatible version — WITH BOTH the range asked
    # for and the version found, because "version mismatch" without the
    # numbers is the OSGi diagnostic this design exists to avoid.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#version'
      in: [ { ref: 'p', pos: 0, provides: [ { name: 's', version: '1.0' } ] }
            { ref: 'c', pos: 1, requires: [ { name: 's', range: '2.1' } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['p'], blocked: [
        { ref: 'c', unmet: 's', why: { kind: 'version', range: '2.1', found: ['1.0'] } } ] }
    }
    # A provider whose attributes fail the match — WITH THE FAILING LEAF.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#match'
      in: [ { ref: 'p', pos: 0, provides: [ { name: 's', attrs: { transactional: false } } ] }
            { ref: 'c', pos: 1, requires: [ { name: 's', match: { transactional: true } } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['p'], blocked: [
        { ref: 'c', unmet: 's'
          why: { kind: 'match', failing: 'transactional', want: true, found: false } } ] }
    }
    # A missing attribute reports `found: null` rather than omitting the
    # key — "the provider does not say" is an answer, and a port that
    # drops the field makes the two cases indistinguishable.
    #
    # A REAL null, not the `__NULL__` marker: this is an `out` comparison
    # and the value genuinely is null. The marker belongs to `match`,
    # where it means "no value" rather than "the value null".
    {
      in: [ { ref: 'p', pos: 0, provides: [ { name: 's' } ] }
            { ref: 'c', pos: 1, requires: [ { name: 's', match: { transactional: true } } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['p'], blocked: [
        { ref: 'c', unmet: 's'
          why: { kind: 'match', failing: 'transactional', want: true, found: null } } ] }
    }
    # A NESTED requirement reports the TOP-LEVEL key that failed, and
    # `found` is the whole value the provider declared under it — the
    # reader needs to see what is actually there, not the one leaf the
    # comparison happened to stop at.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#match-nested'
      in: [ { ref: 'p', pos: 0, provides: [ { name: 's', attrs: { limits: { max: 9 } } } ] }
            { ref: 'c', pos: 1, requires: [ { name: 's', match: { limits: { max: 5 } } } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: ['p'], blocked: [
        { ref: 'c', unmet: 's'
          why: { kind: 'match', failing: 'limits'
                 want: { max: 5 }, found: { max: 9 } } } ] }
    }
    # A provider that is ITSELF blocked, with the chain — so the reader
    # follows one hop rather than re-deriving the graph.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#chain'
      in: [ { ref: 'c', pos: 0, requires: [ { name: 'b' } ] }
            { ref: 'b', pos: 1, provides: [ { name: 'b' } ], requires: [ { name: 'missing' } ] } ]
      out: { resolved: [], blocked: [
        { ref: 'b', unmet: 'missing', why: { kind: 'absent' } }
        { ref: 'c', unmet: 'b', why: { kind: 'blocked', chain: ['b'] } } ] }
    }
    # ONE BLOCKED THING DOES NOT BLOCK THE INNOCENT. This is the whole
    # point of resolving the graph as a phase.
    {
      id: 'graph/blocked#isolated'
      in: [ { ref: 'c', pos: 0, requires: [ { name: 'missing' } ] }
            { ref: 'x', pos: 1 }
            { ref: 'y', pos: 2 } ]
      match: { out: { resolved: ['x', 'y'] } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# resolve — name to candidate module ids (§10.2).
#
#   in: { name, sources? } -> [ids]
#
# PURE, and deliberately so: it returns the ids a host WOULD try, in
# order, and loads nothing. That separation is what lets the corpus pin
# resolution in every language INCLUDING those with no dynamic loading at
# all, and it is why §15.4 puts real module loading in per-port
# integration tests rather than here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: resolve: {

  candidates: set: [
    {
      id: 'resolve/candidates#default', doc: true
      in: { name: 'retry' }
      out: ['@voxgig/plugin-retry', 'voxgig-plugin-retry', 'plugin-retry', 'retry']
    }
    {
      in: { name: 'retry', sources: [ { kind: 'module', prefix: ['x-'] } ] }
      out: ['x-retry']
    }
    # ORDER IS THE ANSWER, not the set: the first id that loads wins, so
    # a port returning the same ids in a different order resolves a
    # different module.
    {
      in: { name: 'r', sources: [ { kind: 'module', prefix: ['a-', 'b-'] } ] }
      out: ['a-r', 'b-r']
    }
    { in: { name: 'r', sources: [ { kind: 'path', dir: './plugin' } ] }, out: ['./plugin/r'] }
    { in: { name: 'r', sources: [ { kind: 'path', dir: './plugin/' } ] }, out: ['./plugin/r'] }
    # Several sources concatenate in declared order.
    {
      in: { name: 'r', sources: [ { kind: 'path', dir: './p' }, { kind: 'module', prefix: ['m-'] } ] }
      out: ['./p/r', 'm-r']
    }
    # Duplicates collapse, keeping the FIRST occurrence — trying the
    # same id twice is not wrong, it is just noise in the answer.
    {
      in: { name: 'r', sources: [ { kind: 'module', prefix: ['a-', 'a-'] } ] }
      out: ['a-r']
    }
    # A SCOPED NAME RESOLVES VERBATIM ONLY (§10.2), whatever the
    # sources say — it is already a package id, and prefixing it
    # produces `@voxgig/plugin-@acme/thing`, which is not a thing that
    # can exist. The default sources are the case that matters: a port
    # honouring this only when the prefix list happens to be empty
    # passes the second entry and fails the first.
    {
      id: 'resolve/candidates#scoped-verbatim'
      in: { name: '@acme/thing' }
      out: ['@acme/thing']
    }
    {
      in: { name: '@acme/thing', sources: [ { kind: 'module', prefix: ['x-'] } ] }
      out: ['@acme/thing']
    }
    {
      in: { name: '@acme/thing', sources: [ { kind: 'path', dir: './p' } ] }
      out: ['@acme/thing']
    }
  ]


  # `from` BYPASSES CANDIDATE GENERATION ENTIRELY (§10.2). A MODULE PATH
  # IS NOT A NAME: the ref grammar starts a name with a letter or `@`,
  # so `./local/thing` is not a ref and never reaches this function at
  # all. seneca allows a path where a plugin name goes; this design
  # deliberately does not, because a ref is an ADDRESS WITHIN A HOST and
  # a path is a LOCATION ON A DISK.
  from: set: [
    { id: 'resolve/from#verbatim', doc: true, in: './local/thing', out: ['./local/thing'] }
    { in: '/abs/path', out: ['/abs/path'] }
    { in: '../up/one', out: ['../up/one'] }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# point — the three kinds, the four hook modes, composition, shadowing
# and exclusivity (§6).
#
# DRIVER. A PLUGIN NEVER MUTATES THE HOST — that inversion is what makes
# deactivation possible, and every entry here is really a test of it:
# sdkgen's `utility.fetcher = wrapped` is not undoable, but "this instance
# holds slot 3 of the request chain" is undoable in O(1).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: point: {

  # `hook` — fan-out. Every live binding runs, in resolved order (§7),
  # and return values are ignored. The probe counts its calls, so the
  # observable shows how many bindings actually ran.
  hook: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/hook#fanout', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { p: { kind: 'hook' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'p' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 1 } }
    }
    # A DEACTIVATED instance does not run. This is the whole point of
    # the inversion: removing a binding is removing a slot, not undoing
    # a mutation nobody recorded.
    {
      id: 'point/hook#deactivated'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { p: { kind: 'hook' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'p' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } }
    }
    # An unknown point is an error, not a silent no-op.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'host', points: {} }, { do: 'emit', point: 'nope' } ]
      err: 'plugin_point_unknown'
    }
    # Emitting on a chain point is a KIND error — the two are not
    # interchangeable and a host that confuses them gets told.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain' } } }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'c' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_point_kind'
    }
  ]


  # `bail` — stops at the first binding that RETURNS A VALUE. The
  # "handled, stop" case. A NULL DECLINES: a binding that has nothing to
  # say returns nothing, and the next one gets its turn.
  bail: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/bail#stops', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'hook', mode: 'bail' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'A' } }
    }
    # With no bindings, nothing is returned — absent, not an error.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'hook', mode: 'bail' } } }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: '__NULL__' } }
    }
    # A NULL RETURN DECLINES, and the next binding gets its turn.
    #
    # §6.1: JavaScript can tell `null` from `undefined` and almost
    # nothing else in the target set can, so a canonical that stopped on
    # `null` had a rule go, python and ruby could not implement - and
    # each of them had already, silently, implemented the other one. This
    # entry is what makes the two readings disagree out loud.
    {
      id: 'point/bail#null-declines', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'hook', mode: 'bail' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: null } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'B' } }
    }
    # ...and a FALSE is a value. The rule is about absence, not about
    # falsiness, which is where a port reaching for its own truthiness
    # would land.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'hook', mode: 'bail' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: false } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'emit', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: false } }
    }
  ]


  # `chain` — composition. b1(b2(b3(base))), FIRST BINDING OUTERMOST
  # (§6.2). The probe prepends its `wrap` string, so the result spells
  # out the nesting order rather than merely asserting it.
  chain: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/chain#outermost', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'ABx' } }
    }
    # Band order decides nesting: lower band is OUTERMOST, the same
    # direction §7 gives, so one rule covers hooks and chains.
    {
      id: 'point/chain#band'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' }, order: { band: 10 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' }, order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'BAx' } }
    }
    # With no bindings the base is called directly.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain' } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'x' } }
    }
    # Deactivating a link RECOMPOSES — the composition is recomputed on
    # any change to the live set, not cached at startup (§6.2).
    {
      id: 'point/chain#recompose'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { c: { kind: 'chain' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Bx' } }
    }
  ]


  # `provider` — at most one live implementation, and THE LOSERS ARE
  # VISIBLE rather than silently ignored (§6.3).
  #
  # The winner is the HIGHEST band, ties by ref sort — the opposite
  # direction from hook and chain, which run lowest first. Two rules,
  # kept visibly different rather than one derived from the other by a
  # reader who then gets it backwards.
  provider: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/provider#one', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'A' } }
    }
    # Highest band wins.
    {
      id: 'point/provider#highest'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' }, order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' }, order: { band: 9 } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'B' } }
    }
    # And the loser is LISTED, not dropped.
    {
      id: 'point/provider#shadowed'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' }, order: { band: 1 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' }, order: { band: 9 } }
        { do: 'shadowed', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['provider$a'] } }
    }
    # AT EQUAL BANDS THE TIE BREAKS BY REF SORT, NOT BY DECLARATION
    # ORDER — the one place §6.3 does not fall through to `pos`, because
    # a provider is picked from a set rather than composed into a
    # sequence, and "whichever loaded first wins" is not a rule anyone
    # can reason about across twenty ports.
    #
    # `provider$b` is declared FIRST here on purpose: a port breaking the
    # tie by declaration order answers 'B' and passes every other entry
    # in this group. Found by the python port (§18, P4).
    {
      id: 'point/provider#tie-by-ref'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'A' } }
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'shadowed', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['provider$b'] } }
    }
    # With no binding the host's default answers.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider', default: 'D' } } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'D' } }
    }
    # `exclusive: true` makes a SECOND binding an error instead of a
    # shadow — for a host that would rather fail than pick.
    {
      id: 'point/provider#exclusive'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider', exclusive: true } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'B' } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_point_exclusive'
    }
    # One binding is fine on an exclusive point.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider', exclusive: true } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v', value: 'A' } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'A' } }
    }
  ]


  # POSITION VERIFICATION (§6.6). Station found that a plugin can need
  # to KNOW it is in the right place: its middleware must sit
  # immediately outside the base transport or its "wire truth" events
  # are fiction.
  #
  # `outermost` and `innermost` are NOT index 0 and index count-1 read
  # the other way round — §6.2 composes b1(b2(b3(base))) with the FIRST
  # binding OUTERMOST. Getting that backwards is the exact error the
  # positional pin vocabulary exists to prevent, so both flags are
  # pinned against a chain with a known composition.
  #
  # THE HOST DOES NOT POLICE THIS; it makes the fact available. A plugin
  # that requires a position it did not get fails loudly rather than
  # reporting nonsense, and that is the plugin's call because only it
  # knows what its position means. Verification tells a plugin it was
  # misplaced; a pin stops the misplacement from being expressible. The
  # two are not substitutes.
  position: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/position#outermost', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' } }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'position', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { index: 0, count: 2, outermost: true, innermost: false } } }
    }
    {
      id: 'point/position#innermost'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' } }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$b', method: 'position', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { index: 1, count: 2, outermost: false, innermost: true } } }
    }
    # A LONE binding is both, which a port special-casing one flag gets
    # wrong.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'position', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { index: 0, count: 1, outermost: true, innermost: true } } }
    }
    # And the composition AGREES with the flags — the same chain, read
    # both ways, so a port cannot satisfy one and invert the other.
    {
      id: 'point/position#agrees-with-composition'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', options: { wrap: 'B' } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'ABx' } }
    }
  ]


  # BINDINGS GO LIVE ONLY WHEN ACTIVATION SUCCEEDS (§8.1). A `loaded`
  # instance has DECLARED its bindings and the host has not inserted
  # them, which is what makes `deactivate` a pure removal and `activate`
  # a pure insertion, with no discovery in between.
  insert: set: [
    {
      id: 'point/insert#loaded-not-bound', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { p: { kind: 'hook' } } }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: [] } }
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { p: { kind: 'hook' } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$a'] } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# export — keying, aliasing, ambiguity (§11).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: export: {

  key: set: [
    {
      id: 'export/key#qualified', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'export', key: 'probe$a/client' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$a' } }
    }
    # A key nothing exports is absent, not an error.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'export', key: 'probe$a/nope' } ]
      match: { out: { result: '__NULL__' } }
    }
    # EXPORTS OF A `loaded` (not live) INSTANCE ARE VISIBLE (§11). They
    # are declared in `define`, they are data, and hiding them would
    # make the loaded state useless for introspection.
    {
      id: 'export/key#loaded-visible'
      cmd: [ { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'export', key: 'probe$a/client' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$a' } }
    }
  ]


  # THE UNQUALIFIED ALIAS, and the case seneca gets wrong. `probe/client`
  # resolves to the UNTAGGED instance if one exists; if not, and exactly
  # one tagged instance exports the key, to that one; if two do, it is
  # an ERROR rather than silent last-wins — with multi-instance as a
  # headline feature, an ambiguous alias is a defect waiting for
  # production.
  alias: set: [
    {
      id: 'export/alias#untagged-wins', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'export', key: 'probe/client' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe' } }
    }
    # No untagged one, exactly one tagged: that one.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'export', key: 'probe/client' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$a' } }
    }
    # Two tagged and no untagged: ERROR, not last-wins.
    {
      id: 'export/alias#ambiguous'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'export', key: 'probe/client' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_export_ambiguous'
    }
    # The message names every candidate — one name leaves the reader
    # guessing which pair collided.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'export', key: 'probe/client' }
      ]
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/probe\\$a/' } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# depend — requirements, pending, and the reactive half (§11.3).
#
# ACTIVATION IS A STANDING REQUEST, NOT A ONE-SHOT EVENT. That single
# sentence is why `pending` is a state rather than a flag, and every
# entry here is a consequence of it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: depend: {

  pending: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/pending#unmet', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$a', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # THE REQUIREMENT IS ON A CAPABILITY, not on a ref: whichever
    # instance provides `clock` satisfies it.
    {
      id: 'depend/pending#capability'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # AN OPTIONAL REQUIREMENT NEVER BLOCKS.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$a', options: { requires: ['clock'], optional: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # A REF SATISFIES TOO (§11.1), AND THE COMPARISON IS CANONICAL
  # (§4 rule 5).
  #
  # §11.1 opens by saying a dependency is on a capability and NOT on a
  # ref, and then makes this one narrow exception: a host that genuinely
  # needs a specific instance should not have to invent a capability for
  # it. Until this group the rule lived only in a comment in the
  # canonical's source — the design did not state it and no entry
  # reached it, so the whole branch was DEAD CODE against the corpus and
  # deleting it outright passed all 552 entries. Seventeen ports carried
  # it because they copied the canonical, not because anything checked.
  #
  # The canonicalization is the half that was named as a gap, and it is
  # the smaller half: `dep$` and `dep` are the same instance, so a port
  # comparing the requirement string against the registry key without
  # canonicalizing first answers `pending` where every other port
  # answers `live`. Every requirement name elsewhere in the corpus is
  # already canonical, which is why nothing saw it.
  byref: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/byref#satisfies', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['dep$p'] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'live', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # THE NON-CANONICAL SPELLING OF THE SAME REF. `dep$` parses to the
    # empty tag and formats back to `dep`, which is the only way two
    # spellings of one ref can differ (§4) — so this is the entry, and
    # there is no other, that a port dropping the canonicalization fails.
    {
      id: 'depend/byref#canonical-comparison'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep', definition: 'dep' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['dep$'] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep': 'live', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # LIVE, exactly as a capability is. A declared instance is a map
    # entry and no executed code (§5.1), and it can no more satisfy a
    # requirement than a capability nobody has provided yet.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['dep$p'] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'declared', 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # ...and `loaded` is not enough either, which is the line that makes
    # this the same rule as the capability one rather than a second one.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['dep$p'] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded', 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # LOSING IT CASCADES, like any other requirement. Without this the
    # ref path could be satisfied once at activation and never rechecked.
    {
      id: 'depend/byref#lost'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['dep$p'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded', 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
  ]


  # THE HOST ACTIVATES IT THE MOMENT THE REQUIREMENT ARRIVES, WITHOUT
  # BEING ASKED AGAIN. Reconciliation is EAGER — it settles by running
  # to a fixed point, not by scheduling.
  arrive: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/arrive#auto', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$c' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live', 'dep$p': 'live' } } }
    }
    # A CHAIN settles in one pass rather than needing N calls.
    {
      id: 'depend/arrive#chain'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['b'] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$c' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['a'], provides: [ { name: 'b' } ] } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$b' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'live', 'dep$b': 'live', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # REACTIVE DEACTIVATION, and its recursion. A live instance whose
  # requirement is LOST goes back to `pending` — and so do its own
  # consumers, because the loss propagates.
  lost: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/lost#back-to-pending', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'pending', 'dep$p': 'loaded' } } }
    }
    # Recursively, through the consumer's own consumers.
    {
      id: 'depend/lost#recursive'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['a'], provides: [ { name: 'b' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['b'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$b': 'pending', 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # AND IT RECOVERS. The standing request was never withdrawn, so the
    # provider returning brings the whole chain back without anyone
    # asking again.
    {
      id: 'depend/lost#recovers'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['a'], provides: [ { name: 'b' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['b'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'live', 'dep$b': 'live', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # A `dynamic` consumer SAID IN WRITING that it can survive the
    # provider going away, so it stays live (§11.3). Static is the
    # default because most plugins cannot, and the cost of wrongly
    # assuming they can is a live instance holding a dead reference.
    {
      id: 'depend/lost#dynamic-stays'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'], policy: 'dynamic' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # An OPTIONAL requirement never causes a loss either.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'], optional: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # `host.capability(name)` returns the live providers, best first — the
  # same rank the `capability` section pins as pure data, now observed
  # through a host so the two cannot drift.
  select: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/select#rank', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$lo', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$hi', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.9' } ] } }
        { do: 'capability', name: 's' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['dep$hi', 'dep$lo'] } }
    }
    # Deactivating one of two providers moves nobody: ANY live instance
    # providing it satisfies a requirement for it.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p1', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p2', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p1' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # §11.4: ALWAYS-RELUCTANT. "A satisfied requirement is not re-bound
    # while it stays satisfied" - so a BETTER provider arriving after
    # the consumer is live does not take it, and losing the one it
    # actually selected still restarts it.
    #
    # THE LOG IS THE DISCRIMINATOR. A host that re-ranks on every
    # question treats `dep$c` as bound to `dep$hi` the moment `dep$hi`
    # goes live, so deactivating `dep$lo` moves nothing and the log ends
    # at `dep$lo:deactivate` - the consumer silently keeps a reference
    # to a provider it was never activated against. The statuses are
    # identical in both readings, which is why this cannot be asserted
    # on `status`.
    {
      id: 'depend/select#reluctant', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$lo', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 's', range: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$hi', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.9' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$lo' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$lo:define', 'dep$lo:activate',
        'dep$c:define', 'dep$c:activate',
        'dep$hi:define', 'dep$hi:activate',
        'dep$c:deactivate', 'dep$lo:deactivate', 'dep$c:activate'
      ] } }
    }
    # ...and under `hold` the same fact is an error rather than a
    # cascade: the HOLDER of `dep$lo` is `dep$c`, because that is the
    # provider `dep$c` actually selected. A re-ranking host names
    # `dep$hi` instead and lets `dep$lo` go.
    {
      id: 'depend/select#reluctant-holds', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$lo', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 's', range: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$hi', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.9' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$hi' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$hi': 'loaded', 'dep$c': 'live', 'dep$lo': 'live' } } }
    }
    # ...and a selection belongs to ONE activation. After the consumer
    # goes down and comes back, it ranks afresh and takes `dep$hi`, so
    # `dep$hi` is now the one that cannot be deactivated under `hold`.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$lo', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 's', range: '2.1' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$hi', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's', version: '2.9' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$c' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$c' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$hi' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_held'
    }
  ]

  # CONSUMERS GO DOWN FIRST, NOT AFTERWARDS (§11.3). The cascade is part
  # of the provider's own deactivation and runs BEFORE the provider's
  # `deactivate` callback and scope unwind, so a consumer's teardown can
  # still call the thing it depends on — flushing a buffer to the store
  # it is about to lose is exactly what a `deactivate` callback is for.
  #
  # The callback log is what makes this checkable rather than asserted:
  # a port that cascades afterwards produces the same STATUSES and a
  # different ORDER, so only the log can tell the two apart.
  # A CASCADED CONSUMER THAT FAILS ITS OWN TEARDOWN LANDS IN `failed`.
  #
  # §5.2: "Any failure during a transition - a lifecycle callback
  # raising, or a ledger entry raising - lands the instance in `failed`."
  # The cascade discarded the error and marked the consumer `pending`,
  # which handed it straight back to `reconcile`: the moment the provider
  # returned, an instance whose teardown had failed was activated again.
  # Found by review of the go port.
  cascadefail: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/cascadefail#consumer', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$c', options: { requires: ['s'], fail: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded', 'noisy$c': 'failed' } } }
    }
    # ...and it is NOT revived when the provider comes back, which is the
    # whole reason `failed` is not `pending`.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 's' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'noisy$c', options: { requires: ['s'], fail: 'deactivate' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'dep$p' }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'dep$p': 'live', 'noisy$c': 'failed' } } }
    }
  ]

  cascade: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/cascade#consumer-first', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$p:define', 'dep$p:activate', 'dep$c:define', 'dep$c:activate'
        'dep$c:deactivate', 'dep$p:deactivate'
      ] } }
    }
    # DEEPEST-FIRST through a chain: c depends on b depends on a, so
    # tearing down a runs c, then b, then a.
    {
      id: 'depend/cascade#deepest-first', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: ['x'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['y'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$a:define', 'dep$a:activate', 'dep$b:define', 'dep$b:activate'
        'dep$c:define', 'dep$c:activate'
        'dep$c:deactivate', 'dep$b:deactivate', 'dep$a:deactivate'
      ] } }
    }
    # `close` INHERITS THE ORDER, which is what makes apply's
    # reverse-load-order teardown safe even when a document happens to
    # list a consumer before its provider. Here the provider sorts first,
    # so a naive reverse-sorted close would tear it down before its
    # consumer.
    {
      id: 'depend/cascade#close-inherits', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$z', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'close' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$a:define', 'dep$a:activate', 'dep$z:define', 'dep$z:activate'
        'dep$z:deactivate', 'dep$z:close', 'dep$a:deactivate', 'dep$a:close'
      ] } }
    }
  ]

  # POLICY IS PER REQUIREMENT (§11.3), and the instance-level spelling is
  # a document-side default for all of them. Only the per-requirement
  # form can say "static on the store, dynamic on the metrics", which is
  # the ordinary case rather than an exotic one.
  policy: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#per-requirement-dynamic', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 'clock', policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # THE CASE THE INSTANCE-LEVEL SPELLING CANNOT EXPRESS: one instance,
    # two requirements, different policies. Losing the dynamic one leaves
    # it live.
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#mixed-keeps-live-on-dynamic-loss', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$s', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'store' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$m', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'metrics' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [
          { name: 'store' }
          { name: 'metrics', policy: 'dynamic' }
        ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$m' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # ...and losing the STATIC one restarts it, same instance, same run
    # shape. The pair is the point: one entry alone cannot show that the
    # two requirements are read separately.
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#mixed-restarts-on-static-loss', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$s', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'store' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$m', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'metrics' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [
          { name: 'store' }
          { name: 'metrics', policy: 'dynamic' }
        ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$s' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # CARDINALITY ALONE GATES ACTIVATION, NOT POLICY. `dynamic` is a
    # statement about surviving a SWAP, not about starting with nothing
    # to call, so a mandatory-dynamic consumer still waits in `pending`
    # for its first provider. A port that conflated the two would let it
    # activate holding no reference at all.
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#dynamic-still-gates-first-activation', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 'clock', policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
    # An OPTIONAL STATIC requirement never gates activation, but a change
    # still DEACTIVATES AND REACTIVATES — the third cell of §11.3's
    # matrix, and the one that is neither "gates" nor "ignores".
    #
    # The reactivation is the half that is easy to miss, and it is not
    # cosmetic: the consumer released the old provider's resources on the
    # way down and takes fresh ones on the way back up, which is the
    # whole reason the cell restarts rather than merely notifying. It
    # ends `live` with its scope reopened, having been down in between.
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#optional-static-restarts', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 'clock', optional: true } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: {
        status: { 'dep$c': 'live' }
        log: [
          'dep$p:define', 'dep$p:activate', 'dep$c:define', 'dep$c:activate'
          'dep$c:deactivate', 'dep$p:deactivate', 'dep$c:activate'
        ]
      } }
    }
    # ...and the fourth cell: OPTIONAL DYNAMIC is a notification and
    # nothing else, so no callback runs on the consumer at all.
    {
      id: 'depend/policy#optional-dynamic-is-untouched', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: [ { name: 'clock', optional: true, policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$p:define', 'dep$p:activate', 'dep$c:define', 'dep$c:activate'
        'dep$p:deactivate'
      ] } }
    }
  ]

  # A cycle through RESTART-CAUSING requirements is
  # `plugin_dependency_cycle`, detected AT LOAD — because the failure it
  # describes is a non-terminating reconcile, and the only safe time to
  # report that is before it starts.
  cycle: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#mutual-mandatory', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ], requires: ['y'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: ['x'] } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_cycle'
    }
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#three-node'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ], requires: ['z'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: ['x'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'z' } ], requires: ['y'] } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_cycle'
    }
    # STATIC OPTIONAL EDGES ARE RESTART-CAUSING, so they cycle too. An
    # earlier draft of §11.3 excluded every optional edge and thereby
    # admitted the non-terminating case it was trying to permit — this
    # entry is the one that catches that reading.
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#static-optional-still-cycles', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ], requires: [ { name: 'y', optional: true } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: [ { name: 'x', optional: true } ] } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_cycle'
    }
    # ONLY DYNAMIC OPTIONAL EDGES ARE EXCLUDED, and they are the ones the
    # exclusion was for: two plugins that optionally and dynamically
    # consume each other both activate happily, neither gates on the
    # other, nothing restarts, so nothing oscillates.
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#dynamic-optional-is-permitted', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ], requires: [ { name: 'y', optional: true, policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: [ { name: 'x', optional: true, policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'live', 'dep$b': 'live' } } }
    }
    # A MANDATORY DYNAMIC edge still cycles: it gates activation, so a
    # mutual pair still deadlocks even though neither would restart.
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#mandatory-dynamic-still-cycles'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ], requires: [ { name: 'y', policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: [ { name: 'x', policy: 'dynamic' } ] } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_cycle'
    }
    # A chain is not a cycle, and this is the entry that stops a port
    # from passing the four above by rejecting every requirement graph.
    {
      id: 'depend/cycle#chain-is-not-a-cycle', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'x' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$b', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'y' } ], requires: ['x'] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['y'] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$a': 'live', 'dep$b': 'live', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]

  # `makeHost({dependency: 'hold'})` restores the strict reading (§11.3).
  # NOT the default, because a station that cannot swap a provider
  # without a restart has lost the argument for having a plugin system.
  hold: set: [
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#ad-hoc-deactivate-is-refused', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_held'
    }
    # The error names the HOLDERS, because "something needs this" without
    # saying what is the diagnostic §12 exists to prevent.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#names-the-holders', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      err: true
      match: { err: { message: '/holders=\\["dep\\$c"\\]/' } }
    }
    # A MANDATORY-DYNAMIC consumer HOLDS. §11.3's word is `required`,
    # which is cardinality, not policy: `dynamic` promises the consumer
    # survives a SWAP, and under `hold` there is no swap - the provider
    # goes and the consumer falls back to `pending`, which is exactly
    # what the policy exists to prevent. Reading the holders off the
    # cascade's restart-causing set excluded this consumer entirely.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#mandatory-dynamic-holds', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'], policy: 'dynamic' } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_dependency_held'
    }
    # ...and an OPTIONAL consumer does NOT hold, whatever its policy. It
    # said in writing that it does not need the thing; `hold` refusing a
    # deactivation on its behalf is the policy speaking for a plugin
    # against that plugin's own declaration. Optional-STATIC is the case
    # that changed: it restarts, so the cascade's set contained it, and
    # `hold` inherited a holder the design never called required.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#optional-does-not-hold', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'], optional: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded', 'dep$c': 'live' } } }
    }
    # An instance nothing holds deactivates normally under `hold`: the
    # policy is a guard on removing something depended upon, not a
    # freeze.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#unheld-deactivates-normally'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded' } } }
    }
    # THE HOLD CHECK IS A GUARD ON AD-HOC DEACTIVATION, NOT ON
    # COORDINATED TEARDOWN. `close()` is removing the holders too, so it
    # is suspended for exactly those holders — otherwise `close()` under
    # `hold` would raise on the first provider it reached whenever a
    # document happened to list a consumer after it, which is the policy
    # refusing to allow the one teardown it has no reason to object to.
    # The consumers-first order still holds.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#close-is-coordinated', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', dependency: 'hold' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$a', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$z', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'close' }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'dep$a:define', 'dep$a:activate', 'dep$z:define', 'dep$z:activate'
        'dep$z:deactivate', 'dep$z:close', 'dep$a:deactivate', 'dep$a:close'
      ] } }
    }
    # Under the DEFAULT policy the same ad-hoc deactivation succeeds and
    # cascades. Without this, every `hold` entry above would pass on a
    # port that refused the deactivation unconditionally.
    {
      id: 'depend/hold#restart-is-the-default', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$p', definition: 'dep', options: { provides: [ { name: 'clock' } ] } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'dep$c', definition: 'dep', options: { requires: ['clock'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'dep$p' }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'dep$p': 'loaded', 'dep$c': 'pending' } } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# declare — `declared` costs nothing, plus AUTO-TAG and `seq` (§5.1, §9.1).
#
# The last two arrived here by correction: §15.3 originally assigned them to
# `ref` and marked that section pure, and neither is reachable from the four
# pure functions. Auto-tagging must know what is already declared and `seq`
# is a counter the host holds, so both need a driver (doc/plan/handover.md §5).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: declare: {

  # `declared` COSTS NOTHING: twenty lazy instances are twenty map
  # entries and no executed code, which is the property station needs
  # and the reason the state exists at all.
  # `options: {}` CLEARS, rather than being read as "no options given".
  # `load` replaces the declared options when the spec carries them, and
  # a port testing TRUTHINESS rather than PRESENCE cannot express
  # "clear them all" - python's `if spec.get('options')` is false for an
  # empty dict. Found by review of the python port.
  clear: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/clear#empty-options', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a', options: {} }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { arg: 'x', do: 'chain', point: 'c' }
      ]
      # The wrap default is ':' - so the cleared options are in force.
      match: { out: { result: ':x' } }
    }
    # ...while OMITTING options leaves the declared ones alone, which is
    # the distinction truthiness cannot draw.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a', options: { wrap: 'A' } }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { arg: 'x', do: 'chain', point: 'c' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Ax' } }
    }
  ]


  # A MALFORMED REF IS `plugin_bad_name` EVERYWHERE, including on the
  # introspection path. `host.instance()` canonicalizes, so it raises -
  # and a port whose lookup swallowed the parse failure reported an
  # ordinary "no such instance" instead, which is a different answer to a
  # different question. Found by review of the go port.
  lookup: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/lookup#malformed', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'seq', ref: 'bad name' } ]
      err: 'plugin_bad_name'
    }
    # A WELL-FORMED ref that is simply absent is not an error - it is the
    # ordinary "nothing here" answer, and the pair is what keeps the two
    # distinguishable.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'seq', ref: 'probe$nope' } ]
      match: { out: { result: '__NULL__' } }
    }
  ]


  free: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/free#nothing-runs', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: {
          'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' }, 'probe$b': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared', 'probe$b': 'declared' }
             open: 0, log: [], result: null }
    }
    # `start: eager` (the default) does run it.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } } ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' } } }
    }
    # `active: false` BARS it: declared, and `ready` on it fails rather
    # than quietly doing nothing.
    {
      id: 'declare/free#barred'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' }, log: [] } }
    }
    # ...AND THE ENTRY ABOVE NEVER CALLED `ready`. Its comment claimed
    # the bar held and only asserted that one apply skipped the ref, so
    # a host keeping the flag nowhere passed it — and the config switch
    # §17.1 exists for (stripe$test off in prod) could be turned back on
    # by any later `ready`.
    {
      id: 'declare/free#barred-stays-barred', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_inactive'
    }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ], err: 'plugin_inactive' }
    # ...and a document that turns it back on clears the bar. A bar that
    # could not be lifted would be a worse bug than one that never held.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # AUTO-TAGGING IS EXPLICIT (§4 rule 3): `tag: '?'` assigns the LOWEST
  # UNUSED POSITIVE INTEGER and returns the assigned pair.
  autotag: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/autotag#first', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe', tag: '?' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$1' } }
    }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe', tag: '?' }, { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe', tag: '?' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$2' } } }
    # LOWEST UNUSED, not next-highest: a gap is filled. A counter-based
    # implementation passes the two entries above and fails this one.
    {
      id: 'declare/autotag#fills-gap'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$1' }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$3' }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe', tag: '?' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$2' } }
    }
    # It skips an existing explicit tag rather than colliding with it.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$1' }, { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe', tag: '?' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 'probe$2' } }
    }
  ]


  # `seq` DISTINGUISHES ONE INCARNATION FROM THE NEXT, and `pos` is the
  # document position. COLLAPSING THEM WOULD LET A RE-APPLIED DOCUMENT
  # SILENTLY REORDER A CHAIN (§4 rule 4), which is why they are two
  # numbers and why this group asserts them separately.
  seq: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/seq#advances', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$b' }, { do: 'seq', ref: 'probe$b' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 1 } }
    }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'seq', ref: 'probe$a' } ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } } }
    # A RE-DECLARED instance gets a FRESH seq — it is a new incarnation.
    {
      id: 'declare/seq#fresh-on-reload'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'seq', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 2 } }
    }
    # And `pos` DOES NOT MOVE with it. This pair is the whole reason the
    # two numbers exist: a counter-based tie-break would sort the
    # re-declared instance behind everything that never moved.
    {
      id: 'declare/seq#pos-stable'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$a' }, { ref: 'probe$b' } ] } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$a', active: false }, { ref: 'probe$b' } ] } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$a' }, { ref: 'probe$b' } ] } }
        { do: 'pos', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } }
    }
  ]


  # `define` REGISTERS, AND THE DEFINITION NEED NOT BE NAMED AFTER THE
  # PROBE BACKING IT (DOCS.md §4.2). `probe` names the catalog entry whose
  # callbacks are used; `name` is what the definition is called, and a ref
  # resolves by NAME. Ten entries already passed `probe` beside an
  # IDENTICAL `name`, so nothing distinguished the two keys and a driver
  # ignoring `probe` outright passed every one of them — which every
  # driver did, `define` having been a no-op until this group.
  register: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/register#probe-differs-from-name', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter', probe: 'noisy' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'adapter$a', options: { fail: 'define', code: 'probe_boom' } }
      ]
      err: 'probe_boom'
    }
    # The pre-seeded `adapter` is a bare record probe that binds nothing,
    # so a driver that took the name and ignored `probe` answers 'x'.
    {
      id: 'declare/register#backed-by-probe'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter', probe: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'adapter$a' }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ':x', status: { 'adapter$a': 'live' } } }
    }
    # ...and the definition IS named `adapter`, so §10.1's "the definition
    # that comes back must be named what was asked for" is satisfied by
    # the ref rather than by the probe it borrowed.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'adapter', probe: 'probe' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'adapter$a' }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'adapter$a': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # THE OPTION SHAPE IS VALIDATED WHEN IT ENTERS THE CATALOG (§10.1),
  # not when a document exercises the key.
  #
  # `config/optmergebad` pins the DOMAIN — which shapes are rejected —
  # and says in its own comment that it cannot pin the TIMING, because
  # every entry there hands `resolveoptions` a shape already in hand. A
  # port that took an invalid shape into its catalog and raised only at
  # resolution passed all eleven. This group closes that: every entry
  # below stops at `define`, with NO load and NO document, so a port
  # that defers raises nothing and fails rather than passing quietly.
  #
  # Timing is the whole of §9.4's claim. A shape checked at resolution
  # fails in a different place in every host — under the first document
  # that touches the key, which in station is a deploy rather than a
  # startup — and "fails once, and in the same place everywhere" is the
  # property the stated domain exists to deliver.
  shape: set: [
    {
      id: 'declare/shape#at-registration', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'define', name: 'probe', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 0 } } } } ]
      err: 'plugin_shape_invalid'
    }
    # The word form too, so the raise is not one special case of `deep`.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'define', name: 'probe', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': 'merge' } } } ]
      err: 'plugin_shape_invalid'
    }
    # A REJECTED REGISTRATION LEAVES NOTHING BEHIND. Without this, a port
    # could store the definition and then raise, and the next `load`
    # would quietly succeed against a definition the catalog was told to
    # refuse. `catch` lets the run continue past the raise so the second
    # command can ask.
    {
      id: 'declare/shape#rejected-not-registered'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'shaped', shape: { m: { '$MERGE': { deep: 0 } } }, catch: true }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'shaped$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_unknown_definition'
    }
    # ...AND A VALID SHAPE IS KEPT, not merely inspected and dropped.
    # §10.1: "a catalog entry carries the definition's option shape, and
    # it is readable before the definition is loaded". Validating at
    # registration and discarding would pass every entry above while
    # making the check pointless, so the shape is followed all the way
    # to a resolved option: the shape's non-directive values are the
    # level-1 defaults (§9.3, `config/optladder#1`), and the probe wraps
    # the `c` chain with `options.wrap`.
    {
      id: 'declare/shape#reaches-resolution', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe', shape: { wrap: 'S' } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Sx' } }
    }
    # The same run WITHOUT the shape, because 'Sx' alone does not say
    # where the S came from.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ':x' } }
    }
    # A $MERGE DIRECTIVE FROM THE CATALOG DRIVES THE MERGE. The pair
    # above only moves a default; this moves the merge rule itself, which
    # is what the shape is for. `provides` is a list, so the library
    # default REPLACES it and the instance's `b` would be the only
    # capability; `append` keeps the document default's `a` as well.
    {
      id: 'declare/shape#directive-at-apply'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'define', name: 'probe', shape: { provides: { '$MERGE': 'append' } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1
            default: { probe: { options: { provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } } }
            instance: { 'probe$x': { options: { provides: [ { name: 'b' } ] } } } } }
        { do: 'capability', name: 'a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: ['probe$x'] } }
    }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1
            default: { probe: { options: { provides: [ { name: 'a' } ] } } }
            instance: { 'probe$x': { options: { provides: [ { name: 'b' } ] } } } } }
        { do: 'capability', name: 'a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: [] } } }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# state — persistence across activation cycles, destruction on unload (§5.4).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: state: {

  survive: set: [
    {
      id: 'state/survive#cycle', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 1 } }
    }
    # Deactivating a rate limiter and reactivating it ten seconds later
    # must not reset its window counters — several cycles, one state.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 2 } }
    }
    # UNLOAD DESTROYS IT. Going back to absent means "as if never
    # loaded", and `close` is what properly undoes `define`.
    {
      id: 'state/survive#unload-destroys'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } }
    }
    # Two instances of one definition do not share state.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'bump' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$b', method: 'count' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 0 } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# resource — the instance scope (§8.3).
#
# THE CORPUS PROVES THE HOST UNWOUND WHAT THE INSTANCE REGISTERED. IT
# CANNOT PROVE THE INSTANCE STOPPED RUNNING (§15.4). A probe that
# squirrels a reference away in a global and keeps using it after
# deactivation would pass every entry here. That is a limit of the
# architecture, not a gap in the corpus, and no amount of test design
# closes it — said here so a green suite cannot be read as claiming it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: resource: {

  scope: set: [
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#difference', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { acquire: 3, release: 1 } }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 2 } }
    }
    # Deactivation unwinds the rest — the scope's job, not a ledger the
    # plugin has to remember to keep.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { acquire: 3, release: 1 } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
    # Releasing everything explicitly leaves nothing to unwind, and
    # unwinding an already-released handle is a NO-OP rather than a
    # double-free — releasing early must not make teardown wrong.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#double-release'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { acquire: 2, release: 2 } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
    # A PARTIAL ACTIVATION ROLLS BACK. The probe acquires before it
    # raises, so a port that unwinds only on the clean path leaks here.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#partial-rollback'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
    # Closing the host unwinds everything still held.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { acquire: 3 } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'close' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0, status: {} } }
    }
    # §8.3 unwinds the scope IN REVERSE REGISTRATION ORDER, "whether an
    # entry came from a host call or from `release`" — and until this
    # entry, NOTHING PINNED THE DIRECTION. An acquired handle is an
    # idempotent counter decrement, so unwinding the handles forwards
    # leaves the same `open` and passes every other entry in this
    # section; the order is observable only through releases that record
    # themselves. Found by the go port (§18, P4).
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#reverse'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { mark: 3 } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'greedy$a', method: 'unwound' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: [2, 1, 0] } }
    }
    # A `release` ENTRY IS A HELD RESOURCE while it is registered and not
    # one afterwards, exactly like an acquired handle: `open` counts what
    # is CURRENTLY held. Registering three and unwinding them must leave
    # the count where it started.
    #
    # The canonical incremented on registration and never decremented,
    # which made every `release` a permanent leak in the counter — and
    # `close()` on a plugin that used one could never reach `open: 0`.
    # Invisible until an entry used `release` at all, which is what these
    # two do.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#release-counts'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { mark: 3 } }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 3 } }
    }
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { mark: 3 } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
    # ...and a host close unwinds them too, which is the case the leak
    # made unreachable.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { acquire: 2, mark: 2 } }
        { do: 'close' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0, status: {} } }
    }
    # `release` outside a lifecycle callback is an error: the scope is
    # the activation's, and a call from anywhere else has no scope to
    # belong to.
    #
    # THIS ROW USED TO ASSERT NOTHING. Every port's `stray` command was a
    # no-op with a comment claiming it called `release`, so the entry
    # stayed green whatever the guard did — or did not do. The probe now
    # exports its own instance api and `stray` calls through it. Found by
    # review of the go port.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#stray'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'stray' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_release_scope'
    }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'call', ref: 'probe$a', method: 'stray', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 1 } } }

    # ...and `acquire` in `define` is the same error, which the guard did
    # NOT catch: it tested "a callback is running" rather than "activate
    # is running", and §8.1 puts capture in `activate`. A scope entry
    # registered in `define` is never unwound at all - `unload` on a
    # merely `loaded` instance does not call it - so this was a
    # permanent leak reachable from ordinary plugin code.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#capture-phase'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { early: 'acquire' } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_release_scope'
    }
    # `release` carries the guard separately, so it needs its own entry:
    # a port fixing one and leaving the other would pass the row above.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy', options: { early: 'release' } }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_release_scope'
    }

    # A FAILING RELEASE. §8.3: "A failing release does not stop the rest.
    # Every entry runs, in reverse order, whatever any of them does; the
    # errors are collected and raised as one `plugin_release_failed`" -
    # and "A failed release ends the instance in `failed`".
    #
    # None of that was implemented, and nothing exercised it: every
    # release in the probe catalog was infallible, so `unwind` swallowing
    # errors was invisible. The host reported a clean `loaded` for an
    # instance that had failed to give back what it held.
    {
      id: 'resource/scope#release-failed'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy'
          options: { mark: 2, markfail: true } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a' }
      ]
      err: 'plugin_release_failed'
    }
    # EVERY entry still runs - the count returns to zero even though both
    # releases raised - and the instance ends in `failed`, not `loaded`.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'greedy$a', definition: 'greedy'
          options: { mark: 2, markfail: true } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'greedy$a', catch: true }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0, status: { 'greedy$a': 'failed' } } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# nest — an instance that is itself a host (§6.5).
#
# THE OUTER OWNS THE INNER'S LIFETIME. Station declares SDK instances as
# plugins, and every generated SDK carries its own plugin system — so a
# station plugin instance is itself a host of feature plugins. Two levels,
# and the outer one owns the inner one.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: nest: {

  lifetime: set: [
    {
      id: 'nest/lifetime#inner-live', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$inner'] } }
        { do: 'inner', ref: 'probe$outer' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'probe$inner': 'live' } } }
    }
    # DEACTIVATING THE OUTER CLOSES THE INNER — in the same reverse
    # unwind as every other resource, because that is what "owns the
    # lifetime" has to mean to be more than a sentence.
    {
      id: 'nest/lifetime#outer-closes-inner'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$inner'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$outer' }
        { do: 'inner', ref: 'probe$outer' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: {} } }
    }
    # The inner host's instances do not appear in the outer's status —
    # two levels, not one flattened namespace.
    {
      id: 'nest/lifetime#isolated'
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$inner'] } } ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$outer': 'live' } } }
    }
  ]


  # A NESTED HOST IS NOT ITSELF AN OPEN RESOURCE, AND THE INNER HOST'S
  # RESOURCES ARE ITS OWN.
  #
  # §6.5 says the outer instance's scope holds the inner host's
  # teardown, and every port implements that. Whether the nest ALSO
  # counts toward the outer's `open` was a free choice nothing pinned:
  # the three entries above assert `result` and `status`, never `open`,
  # so a port that counted the nest reported one more open resource than
  # its neighbours for the same document and stayed green. `open` is the
  # synthetic counter DOCS.md §4.5 puts in the observable precisely so
  # "what is still held" is data rather than a sentence each port words
  # differently, and two ports disagreeing about it is exactly the
  # divergence it exists to expose.
  #
  # It counts NOTHING. The scope entry is a teardown, not an
  # acquisition, and the inner host keeps its own counter — so the
  # outer's `open` is its own `acquire` and no more.
  open: set: [
    {
      id: 'nest/open#nest-is-not-a-resource', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$inner'] } } ]
      match: { out: { open: 1 } }
    }
    # THE SAME NUMBER AS WITHOUT THE NEST, which is the half that says
    # `open: 1` above is the outer's own `acquire` rather than a nest
    # that happens to cost one.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' } ]
      match: { out: { open: 1 } }
    }
    # AND IT DOES NOT SCALE WITH THE INNER HOST'S CONTENTS. Two inner
    # instances acquire two resources IN THE INNER HOST; a port
    # accumulating them into the outer's counter answers 3.
    {
      id: 'nest/open#inner-resources-stay-inner'
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$i1', 'probe$i2'] } } ]
      match: { out: { open: 1 } }
    }
    # ...and the outer's deactivation returns it to zero, closing the
    # inner host on the way. `nest/lifetime#outer-closes-inner` asserts
    # the inner is gone; this asserts nothing was left held.
    {
      id: 'nest/open#unwinds'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$outer', options: { nest: ['probe$i1', 'probe$i2'] } }
        { do: 'deactivate', ref: 'probe$outer' }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0 } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# trace — the lifecycle event records (§14).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: trace: {

  record: set: [
    {
      id: 'trace/record#forward', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }, { do: 'trace' } ]
      match: { out: { result: [
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'define' }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'activate' }
      ] } }
    }
    # `seq` IS ON THE RECORD, which is the whole reason it exists: it
    # distinguishes one incarnation of an instance from the next in a
    # trace, where `pos` would be identical for both.
    {
      id: 'trace/record#seq'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'unload', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'trace' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: [
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'define', seq: 0 }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'activate', seq: 0 }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'deactivate', seq: 0 }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'close', seq: 0 }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'define', seq: 1 }
        { ref: 'probe$a', event: 'activate', seq: 1 }
      ] } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# apply — idempotent document application (§9.6).
#
# `apply` DECLARES EVERYTHING AND ACTIVATES ONLY WHAT ASKED FOR IT, and it
# is RE-RUNNABLE: applying the same document twice changes nothing, which
# is what lets a host reload config without tracking what it already did.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: apply: {

  idempotent: set: [
    {
      id: 'apply/idempotent#twice', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
      ]
      out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' }, open: 1
             log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate'], result: null }
    }
    # The LOG is the assertion, not the status: a port that re-ran
    # `define` would end in the same state and be wrong.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [] } }
    }
    # AND THE OPTIONS REACH THE INSTANCE ON THE FIRST APPLY.
    #
    # No entry anywhere observed an option that arrived through `apply`,
    # and the canonical was silently dropping every one of them: `apply`
    # resolves the options and hands the map to `declare`, which for a
    # ref declared for the first time ADOPTS that map — so the refill
    # afterwards cleared its own source and left the instance with
    # nothing. Applying the same document a SECOND time filled them in,
    # because by then the two maps were distinct. Every existing apply
    # entry passed, because each asserted status, log or open and none
    # asserted a value the options decide.
    #
    # `wrap` is read by the probe's `c` binding at call time, so the
    # chain result is the option, not a copy of it.
    {
      id: 'apply/idempotent#options-first-time', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'W' } } } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Wx' } }
    }
    # ...and are still there after a second, identical apply — the fix
    # must not trade the first apply for the second.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'W' } } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'W' } } } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Wx' } }
    }
    # A re-apply that CHANGES the value still lands, which is what the
    # refill exists for: the binding closed over the map at `define`.
    {
      id: 'apply/idempotent#options-changed'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'W' } } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'Z' } } } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Zx' } }
    }
  ]


  # ADD, REMOVE, PATCH, TOGGLE — the four things a second document can do.
  # §9.6's FIRST CLAUSE, which nothing implemented: "load what is
  # missing, UNLOAD WHAT IS GONE, patch what changed". `apply` walked
  # only the document's own refs, so an instance the new document
  # DROPPED stayed live, with its bindings in every chain and its
  # resources held - in the config-reload case the method exists for.
  # Found by review of the go port.
  gone: set: [
    {
      id: 'apply/gone#dropped', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {}, 'probe$b': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' } } }
    }
    # It is UNLOADED, not merely deactivated: `probe$b` is absent from
    # the status map entirely, and its resource is released.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {}, 'probe$b': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'list' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: { 'probe$b': '__UNDEF__' }, open: 1 } }
    }
    # ...and `close` ran, which is what makes "as if never loaded" true.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: {} } }
      ]
      match: { out: { open: 0, status: {}
                      log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate'
                            'probe$a:deactivate', 'probe$a:close'] } }
    }
    # A ref that was only ever `declared` is left alone rather than
    # unloaded, because there is nothing to undo and `unload` would run
    # `close` on an instance that never ran `define`.
    {
      id: 'apply/gone#declared-untouched'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: {} } }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [], status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' } } }
    }
  ]


  # §9.6's ORDERING: "deactivations and unloads first (reverse load
  # order), then loads, then activations in load order". THREE PHASES,
  # not one interleaved walk - and the log is the only thing that can
  # tell them apart, which is why nothing caught it.
  phases: set: [
    {
      id: 'apply/phases#declare-all-then-activate', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$a' }, { ref: 'probe$b' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$b:define'
                            'probe$a:activate', 'probe$b:activate'] } }
    }
    # Unloads come FIRST and in REVERSE load order, so a consumer
    # declared after its provider goes down before it.
    {
      id: 'apply/phases#reverse-teardown'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$a' }, { ref: 'probe$b' } ] } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: [ { ref: 'probe$c' } ] } }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: ['probe$a:define', 'probe$b:define'
                            'probe$a:activate', 'probe$b:activate'
                            'probe$b:deactivate', 'probe$b:close'
                            'probe$a:deactivate', 'probe$a:close'
                            'probe$c:define', 'probe$c:activate'] } }
    }
  ]

  change: set: [
    # ADD: a new ref appears.
    {
      id: 'apply/change#add', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {}, 'probe$b': {} } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live', 'probe$b': 'live' } } }
    }
    # PATCH: options change without the instance restarting from scratch.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'A' } } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'Z' } } } } }
        { do: 'chain', point: 'c', arg: 'x' }
      ]
      match: { out: { result: 'Zx' } }
    }
    # TOGGLE to inactive RETURNS IT TO `declared`, BY UNLOADING IT
    # (§9.6). There is no loaded->declared transition and there should
    # not be one: going back to `declared` means "as if never loaded",
    # and an instance that has run `define` has state and bindings only
    # `close` can properly undo. Its state is DESTROYED, which is the
    # honest meaning of the change.
    {
      id: 'apply/change#toggle-off'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' }, open: 0 } }
    }
    # And the unload is VISIBLE in the log — `close` ran.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { log: [
        'probe$a:define', 'probe$a:activate', 'probe$a:deactivate', 'probe$a:close' ] } }
    }
    # Toggling to LAZY does the same thing, for the same reason.
    {
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { start: 'lazy' } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' } } }
    }
    # And BACK ON again, from a clean slate.
    {
      id: 'apply/change#toggle-back'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } } } }
        { do: 'apply', doc: { plugin: 1, instance: { 'probe$a': {} } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'live' }, open: 1 } }
    }
  ]


  # A document with a PROFILE selected applies the overlay, and the
  # defaults-after-merge rule holds through `apply` exactly as it does
  # through normalizeconfig — the safety rule is not lost between the
  # pure function and the host that calls it.
  profile: set: [
    {
      id: 'apply/profile#bar-survives', doc: true
      cmd: [
        { do: 'apply', profile: 'prod', doc: {
          plugin: 1
          instance: { 'probe$a': { active: false } }
          profile: { prod: { instance: { 'probe$a': { options: { wrap: 'P' } } } } } } }
      ]
      match: { out: { status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' } } }
    }
  ]
}


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# error — the codes of §12 and the MESSAGE FORMAT.
#
#   plugin/<code>: <text> [<key>=<value> …]
#
# The bracketed detail carries the fields that apply, IN A FIXED ORDER —
# host, ref, name, tag, point, key, capability, range, version, match,
# candidates, cycle, holders, refs, path, cause — omitting those that do
# not, and absent entirely when none do.
#
# THE ORDER IS CONTRACT, not formatting. An earlier draft named six
# fields while other sections promised diagnostics that had nowhere to
# go, which would have left each port inventing its own order and
# breaking message parity. Values render as COMPACT JSON so a value
# containing a space or a bracket cannot break the parse.
#
# Ports compare by CODE, never by wording — a port's language is its own
# business, and pinning the words would make every translation a corpus
# change. The FORMAT is what is pinned, because a parseable message is
# what makes a log searchable across twenty languages.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

primary: error: {

  format: set: [
    # The prefix and code, verbatim.
    {
      id: 'error/format#prefix', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'activate', ref: 'nope$a' } ]
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/^plugin\\/plugin_not_loaded: /' } }
    }
    # The detail bracket, with the field that applies.
    {
      cmd: [ { do: 'activate', ref: 'nope$a' } ]
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/\\[ref="nope\\$a"\\]/' } }
    }
    # Values render as COMPACT JSON — quoted, so a ref containing a
    # space or a bracket cannot break a parser reading the line.
    {
      id: 'error/format#json-values'
      cmd: [ { do: 'emit', point: 'nope' } ]
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/\\[point="nope"\\]/' } }
    }
    # A list field renders as a JSON ARRAY.
    {
      id: 'error/format#list-field'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'probe$b' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ]
      err: true, match: { err: { message: '/cycle=\\[/' } }
    }
  ]


  # The codes themselves, each reached through the path that raises it.
  codes: set: [
    { id: 'error/codes#not-loaded', doc: true
      cmd: [ { do: 'activate', ref: 'nope$a' } ], err: 'plugin_not_loaded' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'load', ref: 'nosuch$a' } ], err: 'plugin_unknown_definition' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'load', ref: 'probe$a', definition: 'other' }
      ], err: 'plugin_ref_duplicate' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'emit', point: 'nope' } ], err: 'plugin_point_unknown' }
    { cmd: [ { do: 'chain', point: 'p' } ], err: 'plugin_point_kind' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a', order: { after: 'probe$a' } }
        { do: 'order', point: 'p' }
      ], err: 'plugin_order_cycle' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { fail: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a', catch: true }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ], err: 'plugin_bad_state' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'load', ref: 'noisy$a', options: { reenter: 'activate' } }
        { do: 'activate', ref: 'noisy$a' }
      ], err: 'plugin_reentrant' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$a' }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'probe$b' }
        { do: 'export', key: 'probe/client' }
      ], err: 'plugin_export_ambiguous' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'host', points: { v: { kind: 'provider', exclusive: true } } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$a', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v' } }
        { do: 'ready', ref: 'provider$b', definition: 'provider', options: { point: 'v' } }
        { do: 'provider', point: 'v' }
      ], err: 'plugin_point_exclusive' }
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'host', reserved: ['station'] }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'station$a' }
      ], err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }

    # ...but THE HOST ITSELF MUST STILL BE ABLE TO DECLARE IT. §9.1:
    # "The host declares those instances itself, after the user merge,
    # and always wins." Every path to `declare` was barred, including the
    # embedding host's, so `reserved: ['station']` made it impossible for
    # station to install the adapter it had reserved the name for -
    # a feature that defeated its own purpose. Found by review of the go
    # port.
    { id: 'error/codes#reserved-host-path'
      cmd: [
        { do: 'host', reserved: ['probe'] }
        { do: 'hostdeclare', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ], match: { out: { result: 'probe$a', status: { 'probe$a': 'declared' } } } }
    # ...while the ordinary path to the same ref stays barred, which is
    # what makes the pair an assertion rather than a bypass.
    { cmd: [
        { do: 'host', reserved: ['probe'] }
        { do: 'declare', ref: 'probe$a' }
      ], err: 'plugin_ref_reserved' }
  ]
}
